<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<system>
    <!-- The librefirewall system description for the current QEMU q35
         bring-up: the two-dataplane-port zero-copy virtio-net forwarding slice
         (virtio 1.0 PCI), plus a dedicated management port. THREE instances of
         the same driver binary, one per port; the two dataplane ones are joined
         through a forwarder PD by one pipeline per direction — three memory
         regions each — and the third hands its frames to a management PD that
         forwards nothing. Each dataplane pipeline's buffer pool is DMA'd into by
         the receiving NIC and out of by the transmitting NIC, so a frame crosses
         the whole system without ever being copied. The session-replication and
         mirror ports of the full port model are not part of this
         slice. A seventh domain, the RECORDER, owns the appliance's virtio-blk
         device and touches no frame: it is joined to the forwarder by a capture
         tap it may only read, and to the management domain by a download
         handover, and it holds no dataplane region of any kind. An eighth
         binary, the HARDWARE PROBE, holds no device and no channel at all: it
         is one of the three domains compiled with the hardfloat SIMD target,
         proves the AES-NI/PCLMULQDQ/XMM profile once at boot, and parks. A
         ninth, the CRYPTOGRAPHY domain, is another: it holds no device and
         no channel either, proves every cryptographic primitive against its
         published vectors on this part, measures what each costs, seeds a
         generator from RDRAND, and parks. A tenth, the STORE domain, is the
         third built with that target and the second to own a block device: it
         holds the appliance's own persistent state on a device of its own,
         mints an identity on a fresh medium or reloads and verifies the one
         already there, reports which appliance this is, and parks. It holds no
         channel either. The protection domains are documented in
         pds/nic-driver, pds/forwarder, pds/management, pds/recorder,
         pds/hardware-probe, pds/crypto and pds/store. -->

    <!-- ===================================================================
         The physical address map is a contract, and this is where it is
         stated. Every region a `setvar region_paddr` names fixes phys_addr, and
         none of those may stop doing so: a driver hands the address to its
         device, and the Microkit tool refuses that pairing without a fixed
         address — "setvar with 'region_paddr' for MR without a specified paddr
         is unsupported on x86-64", because the x86-64 physical memory layout is
         not known at build time (Microkit 2.3.0 manual, SDF `setvar`). Microkit
         therefore cannot place those regions for us; their addresses are chosen
         here, so what makes each one correct belongs here too.

         Two opposite requirements, both satisfied today by one unstated
         quantity — the QEMU memory size:

         * vq0..vq2, blk_dma and blk2_dma, the twelve pipeline regions and the
           two staging windows blk_io and blk2_io
           (0x30000000..0x3110D000) must
           lie INSIDE RAM. They are ordinary memory, and seL4 zeroes a frame
           retyped from a general-purpose untyped but not one retyped from a
           device untyped — which is what an address outside RAM yields. That
           kernel behaviour is the whole of what establishes the
           zero-initialised precondition `virtio::queue::SplitVirtqueue::new`
           and `pd_runtime::attach_region!` both require of their mapping; no
           first-party code re-checks it, and a region placed outside RAM would
           fail as unbacked reads at run time rather than as a build or boot
           error.
         * bar0..bar4 (0x50000000..0x50014000) must lie OUTSIDE RAM, in the q35
           32-bit PCI hole: each driver reprograms its device's 64-bit MMIO BAR
           to that address, so RAM underneath it would be shadowed by the
           device's decode. The two block devices' BARs are the fourth and fifth
           and are placed on exactly the terms the three NICs' are.

         Under `-m 1G` (tools/xtask/src/qemu.rs) low RAM is [0, 0x40000000),
         which puts the first group inside it and the second above it. The
         window either side is narrow and nothing checks it: RAM must now reach
         past 0x3110D000 — 785.05 MiB rather than the 784.80 MiB the recorder's
         device alone needed and the 784.55 MiB the three ports alone did, so the
         store device's staging region NARROWS the lower end of that unasserted
         window by a further 256 KiB and every further device narrows it again —
         while at 1280 MiB or more RAM reaches 0x50000000 and swallows the BAR
         window. Changing `-m`, or targeting hardware with a different memory
         map, means re-deriving this map. README's status records the window as
         load-bearing and unasserted, and this is another change that made it
         tighter rather than the change that closed it.

         The two staging regions are by some way the largest single claims on
         that window — 256 KiB each where every other region added here costs
         four — and they are the first things to shrink if the window ever has to
         be given back. For the recorder's, nothing but how much it can have in
         flight at once depends on the size; for the store's, nothing at all
         does, its whole record being eight sectors — but shrinking it means
         giving one device class two staging bounds, which is a deliberate change
         rather than a free one.

         The two configuration regions (cfg, cfgack) are the exception and are
         deliberately NOT in the packed range, which now ends at 0x3110D000.
         No device DMAs into them, so no driver needs their physical address, so
         no `setvar region_paddr` names them and the tool has no reason to
         demand a fixed one. Leaving it out is not a convenience: an unplaced
         region is one Microkit allocates from general-purpose untyped memory,
         which is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes — so `attach_region!`'s
         zero-initialisation precondition holds by construction for these two,
         rather than by the RAM-membership argument above, which rests on `-m`
         and which nothing checks. Fewer fixed addresses is the stronger
         position here, and the only reason the regions above keep theirs is
         that a device has to be told where they are.
         =================================================================== -->

    <!-- PCI ECAM pages for the pinned devices 00:02.0, 00:03.0 and 00:04.0 —
         the two dataplane ports and the management port. The deployable image
         boots through OVMF, which programs the q35 PCIEXBAR (MMCONFIG base) to
         0xE0000000; the page of device D is at base + (D << 15), which is what
         makes these three addresses a derivation rather than three choices.
         Device MMIO: uncached. -->
    <memory_region name="ecam0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0010000" />
    <memory_region name="ecam1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0018000" />
    <memory_region name="ecam2" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0020000" />
    <!-- And the fourth pinned function, 00:05.0 — the virtio-blk device the
         recorder owns. Same derivation, one slot past the management port:
         0xE0000000 + (5 << 15). The slot is not free-floating either, and the
         one place that could disagree is held to it — `xtask::qemu` builds the
         guest's `-device` arguments from `GuestNic::slot() + 2` for the three
         NICs and pins the data device at `addr=05.0` beside them, so the guest
         topology and this page are one derivation rather than two numbers that
         happen to match. -->
    <memory_region name="ecam3" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0028000" />
    <!-- And the fifth, 00:06.0 — the virtio-blk device the STORE domain owns,
         which is a second block device and not a second view of the recorder's.
         Same derivation, one slot further on: 0xE0000000 + (6 << 15). The slot
         is held to the guest topology from the other side exactly as the
         recorder's is: `xtask::qemu` pins the store device at `addr=06.0`
         beside the recorder's `addr=05.0`, so this page and the guest are one
         derivation rather than two numbers that happen to match. -->
    <memory_region name="ecam4" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0030000" />
    <!-- Where each driver relocates its device's modern MMIO BAR (patched into
         the driver via region_paddr). Packed back to back from the base of the
         q35 32-bit PCI hole, so the three together are 0x50000000..0x5000C000.
         Device MMIO: uncached. -->
    <memory_region name="bar0" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50000000" />
    <memory_region name="bar1" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50004000" />
    <memory_region name="bar2" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50008000" />
    <!-- The block device's BAR, packed immediately after the three NICs', so
         the four together are 0x50000000..0x50010000. Its size is
         `lfw_blk::BAR_WINDOW_SIZE` rather than the drivers'
         `nic_driver_core::bringup::BAR_WINDOW_SIZE`: the two crates are
         independent device classes with independent bounds, and citing one
         crate's constant for the other's window would make a change to either
         silently move the other. That the two numbers agree today is not a fact
         either crate promises. -->
    <memory_region name="bar3" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x5000C000" />
    <!-- The store device's BAR, packed immediately after the recorder's, so the
         five together are 0x50000000..0x50014000. Its size is `lfw_blk`'s for
         the reason `bar3`'s is: the two block devices are one device class, and
         the NIC drivers' constant is a different crate's. -->
    <memory_region name="bar4" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50010000" />

    <!-- The High Precision Event Timer's register block, and the third device
         region in this system.

         Its phys_addr is not ours to choose, unlike vq0/vq1 and the pipeline
         regions above: 0xFED00000 is where every x86 chipset places the block,
         and it is `lfw_hpet::MMIO_BASE`, which that crate forms every register
         address inside. The authoritative source is the ACPI HPET description
         table — and it is unusable here because hardware topology is fixed
         at build time: a base discovered at run time is one no
         statically granted capability could map, so the conventional answer is
         the one that can be checked and the authoritative one is the one that
         cannot.

         SIZE IS A PAGE AND THE BLOCK IS 1 KIB, which is not slack anybody
         chose. A grant is whole pages, so `lfw_hpet::MMIO_REGION_SIZE` is
         `MMIO_LENGTH` rounded up to one and that is what `xtask::sysdesc`
         compares this row against — the same derivation `wire` makes for a log
         region. What keeps the rounding from widening the grant is a
         const-assertion in that crate: 0xFED00000 is page-aligned and the block
         is 0x400 bytes, so the whole of the page beyond the block is the same
         device's decode and no other device sits in it.

         Device MMIO: uncached, on the same footing as the ECAM pages and BAR
         windows above. A counter read through a cached mapping is read once and
         then answered from the cache forever, which is a stopped clock that
         reports itself running — the one failure `lfw_hpet` cannot detect,
         since it distinguishes a stalled counter from a slow one by reading it
         again. -->
    <memory_region name="hpet" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xFED00000" />

    <!-- virtio virtqueues (driver <-> NIC), receive and transmit per port.

         Every map of a virtqueue or a pipeline below states cached="true"
         rather than leaning on the default, because both are DMA memory and
         `virtio::queue` names a cached mapping as a premise of its correctness
         argument: its fences order CPU-visible memory only, which suffices
         because x86 DMA is cache-coherent and the region is cached. A premise a
         crate reasons from is declared where the mapping is granted, not
         inherited from a default that can move. -->
    <memory_region name="vq0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30000000" />
    <memory_region name="vq1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30001000" />
    <memory_region name="vq2" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30002000" />

    <!-- The block device's DMA region, packed immediately after the three
         virtqueues because it is the same kind of memory and is read the same
         way: the request virtqueue at its base, and behind it the sixteen-byte
         header of each in-flight request and the byte the device writes its
         status into. `lfw_blk`'s layout assertions are what place those three
         inside one page; this row is only what makes the page exist.

         It is a DRIVER-PRIVATE region and deliberately not a staging one. No
         payload passes through it, so nothing the recorder records and nothing
         an operator downloads is ever reachable at this address — which is why
         it stays one page while `blk_io` is sixty-four.

         Size is `lfw_blk::DMA_REGION_SIZE`, cached for the reason every
         virtqueue above is, and `xtask::sysdesc` compares both. -->
    <memory_region name="blk_dma" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30003000" />

    <!-- Three regions per forwarding direction (rx driver -> forwarder -> tx
         driver), because a region is the unit of grant: what a domain can reach
         is decided here and not by which fields its code touches. pipe 0 is
         port 0 -> port 1; pipe 1 the reverse.

         Each pool is both NICs' DMA target, so it needs a fixed physical
         address the drivers hand to their devices (region_paddr, below).

         Every size is a `pd_runtime` constant rather than a chosen number:
         `POOL_REGION_SIZE`, `FORWARD_REGION_SIZE` and `RETURN_REGION_SIZE`, each
         its region type's `size_of` rounded up to the mapping granularity. It is
         one value in two places, and `xtask::sysdesc` reads this file back and
         fails the gate on any inequality: moving one side alone would otherwise
         silently truncate the mapping or re-widen the grant.

         Splitting the pool from the rings costs one page. `ForwardRings` is
         4112 bytes and `ReturnRing` 2056; the first needs two pages and the
         second one, so 0x20000 + 0x2000 + 0x1000 is one page more than the
         0x22000 a single region holding all four components rounds up to.
         `pd_runtime` asserts that equality at build time, and that page is what
         keeps the `free` ring out of the forwarder's address space entirely.

         The regions stay packed back to back: 0x31000000 through 0x31046000. -->
    <memory_region name="pool0" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31000000" />
    <memory_region name="fwd0" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31020000" />
    <memory_region name="free0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31022000" />
    <memory_region name="pool1" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31023000" />
    <memory_region name="fwd1" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31043000" />
    <memory_region name="free1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31045000" />

    <!-- The MANAGEMENT PORT'S two pipelines, the same three regions each and the
         same `pd_runtime` constants, packed immediately after the dataplane's:
         0x31046000 through 0x3108C000.

         BOTH PIPELINES ARE NOW USED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the change this
         port's addressing brought, a reviewed capability change. The receive three carry frames
         to the management domain as before; the transmit three now carry the
         replies that domain originates, so the driver binary's transmit side —
         which existed because the binary is port-agnostic and takes one whatever
         port it drives (pds/nic-driver's crate header) — has a producer at last.

         What still differs from the dataplane is the far end of the receive
         pipeline. There is no egress driver to hand a descriptor onward to, so
         the management domain is the end of the journey and produces the return
         itself onto mgmt_rx_free — the same producer/consumer split the dataplane
         already has between its two drivers, with that domain standing where the
         egress driver stands.

         THE TWO POOLS ARE OWNED BY DIFFERENT DOMAINS, in opposite directions,
         and that asymmetry is the whole of why a forged return is refused on
         both. mgmt_rx_pool is the DRIVER's: it receives into it, and the
         management domain maps it READ-ONLY to copy a frame out — a frame this
         appliance was sent is never altered, so read is the whole of the
         authority that reading it needs. mgmt_tx_pool is the MANAGEMENT
         DOMAIN's: it allocates a buffer, writes a reply into it (hence
         read-write) and lends it, and the driver maps it because `TxPath::post`
         writes the 12-byte virtio-net header in front of the frame.

         mgmt_rx_pool was mapped by no protection domain at all until this
         change, and that claim is gone rather than weakened: the grant arrived
         with the code that reads a frame, which is exactly the condition the
         note it replaces set for it. `xtask::sysdesc` carries the new
         mapper set as a rule, read-only side included, so a widening to
         read-write fails the gate at the point the edit is made. -->
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_pool" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31046000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_fwd" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31066000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_free" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31068000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_pool" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31069000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_fwd" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31089000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_free" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x3108B000" />

    <!-- THE BLOCK STAGING WINDOW, and the last region in the packed range:
         0x3108C000 through 0x310CC000.

         It is where every byte the recorder reads off its medium arrives and
         every byte it writes to one is composed, and it is a region of its own
         rather than more of `blk_dma` for the reason each pipeline is three
         regions: the two hold different things and the split is what lets them
         be different sizes and, one day, different grants. `blk_dma` carries
         this driver's bookkeeping — a virtqueue, sixteen bytes of header per
         request and a status byte — and is a page. This carries payload, and it
         is 256 KiB because that is how much a recorder may usefully have in
         flight at once.

         IT NEEDS A FIXED PHYSICAL ADDRESS FOR THE ORDINARY REASON. Every data
         segment of every request names a byte of this region by physical
         address, and the device DMAs to it, so the recorder is told where it is
         (`io_paddr`, below) exactly as each driver is told where its pool is.
         There is no IOMMU on this platform, so that address is an
         address the device may write — which is what `lfw_blk::io::IoRegion`
         exists for: the authority to name a byte of this window exists in one
         shape, an `IoSector` that is in range by construction, so a wrong
         offset is unrepresentable rather than caught.

         ONE MAPPER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF THE ISOLATION. No other domain maps
         it in either direction. The forwarder does not: a capture reaches the
         recorder as an annotation on the `tap` ring, never as a pointer into
         this window, so a compromised forwarder cannot write bytes that will be
         committed to the medium under the appliance's own name. The management
         domain does not either, and that one is worth stating plainly: it is the
         domain a download is answered from, so the obvious shortcut would be to
         let it read the staging window directly. It reads `dl_reply` instead —
         a copy the recorder composed and published — because a management domain
         that could read this region could read whatever the recorder had most
         recently staged, download or not, and one that could write it could put
         bytes of its own choosing on the medium.

         Size is `lfw_blk::BLK_IO_REGION_SIZE`, cached on the same footing as
         every DMA region above, and `xtask::sysdesc` compares both. -->
    <memory_region name="blk_io" size="0x40000" phys_addr="0x3108C000" />

    <!-- The store device's two RAM regions, packed immediately after the
         recorder's staging window, so the packed range now ends at 0x3110D000.

         `blk2_dma` holds the request virtqueue for the second block device and
         is one page for the reason `blk_dma` is: the queue, the per-slot request
         headers and the status bytes fit it, and no payload crosses it.

         `blk2_io` is where every byte of the appliance's own persistent state
         crosses. It is `lfw_blk::BLK_IO_REGION_SIZE` — the same 256 KiB the
         recorder's is — and that is the device class's grant rather than this
         domain's appetite: the store's whole record is eight sectors, and a
         narrower region would be a second staging bound for one device class,
         which `IoRegion::attach`'s contract and `xtask::sysdesc`'s rule for the
         region both hold to one number. It is the largest single claim this
         change makes on the unasserted window above, and it is the first thing
         to shrink if that window ever has to be given back — but shrinking it
         means giving the block device class two staging bounds, which is a
         change to make deliberately rather than to reach for.

         BOTH ARE WITHHELD FROM EVERY OTHER DOMAIN, including the recorder. The
         two block devices are separate authorities: the recorder cannot read or
         write the appliance's identity, and the store domain cannot touch a
         recording. A grant crossing that line would put the private scalar
         within reach of the domain a download request reaches. -->
    <memory_region name="blk2_dma" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x310CC000" />
    <memory_region name="blk2_io" size="0x40000" phys_addr="0x310CD000" />

    <!-- The configuration handover, one region per direction.

         `cfg` carries the validated configuration image and the two generation
         words that publish it; `cfgack` carries what the one consumer has done
         with the offered generation. They are two regions rather than one field
         set for the reason the pipeline is three regions: a region is the unit
         of grant, and only two regions can give each domain write access to
         exactly the direction it speaks in. The config domain cannot forge an
         acknowledgement to release its own generation, and the forwarder cannot
         rewrite the configuration it is about to be judged by.

         `cfg` HAS TWO READERS AND `cfgack` STILL ONE WRITER, and the asymmetry
         is a decision rather than an omission. The management
         domain maps `cfg` read-only — its addressing comes from the
         document — and maps `cfgack` NOT AT ALL, because it is not the
         *consumer* of the two-phase commit. The forwarder is: it reads the
         offered generation, stages a table and acknowledges, and a commit waits
         for that. The management domain reads the COMMITTED generation only
         (`pd_runtime::CommittedReader`), which is a strictly weaker role — it
         cannot delay a commit, cannot refuse one on anybody's behalf, and holds
         no region to forge an acknowledgement in. Granting it `cfgack` would
         make "every consumer has staged" a claim about two domains and hand a
         domain that answers the management-plane attacker the word that releases
         a generation.

         What that costs is stated at pds/management, and it is smaller than it was
         and still real: that domain now holds a channel to the config domain, but
         it carries SUBMISSIONS rather than the commit protocol — the management
         domain still reads the committed generation out of `cfg` and takes no part
         in releasing one. So a commit somebody else provoked still reaches it on
         the next frame or the next answer that wakes it, rather than at the commit.

         Sizes are `wire::CONFIG_REGION_SIZE` and `wire::CONFIG_ACK_REGION_SIZE`,
         on the same footing as the pipeline constants above and held to them
         the same way: `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule for each of these two
         regions and compares it. `ConfigAck` is 8 bytes and rounds to the one
         page it costs.

         `cfg` IS THE ONE REGION HERE RESERVED PAST WHAT IT HOLDS. `ConfigHandover`
         is 14156 bytes and would round to four pages; eight are reserved,
         because this region's size is the one thing in this file that cannot be
         changed locally. It is mapped at a FIXED VIRTUAL ADDRESS in three
         domains, and every region behind it in that window moves when it grows
         — so a configuration image that outgrew its pages would re-lay an
         address map three protection domains have to agree on, in the same
         change as whatever grew it. That has now happened once: the filter's 256
         rule slots took the image from 840 bytes to 14156, and the reservation
         went from four pages to eight in the same change, so the next entity to
         be configured lands inside a map already laid. Eight pages is the
         reservation `wire` states and this file states the consequence of:
         `cfgack` and `clock` sit at 0x3_008_000 and 0x3_009_000 rather than one
         page apart, and the window from 0x3_000_000 is claimed to 0x3_00a_000.

         Neither carries phys_addr; see the address-map block above on why that
         is the stronger position for a region no device is told about. -->
    <memory_region name="cfg" size="0x8000" />
    <memory_region name="cfgack" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- The configuration SUBMISSION channel, one region per direction, and the
         path by which a document reaches this appliance at all.

         `cfg_request` carries what the management domain is asking — an operation
         word, a length, and up to `wire::MAX_DOCUMENT_BYTES` of document — and
         `cfg_reply` carries the answer: a status from a closed set, the generation
         now in force, a reject reason and the number that locates it, and, for a
         read, the document the appliance is running. MANAGEMENT maps `cfg_request`
         READ-WRITE and `cfg_reply` READ-ONLY; the CONFIG domain maps
         `cfg_request` READ-ONLY and `cfg_reply` READ-WRITE. Each writes exactly
         the direction it speaks in, which is `dl_request`/`dl_reply`'s split and
         is here for a sharper reason than there.

         WHY THE DIRECTION OF TRUST RUNS THIS WAY. The management domain is the
         one an attacker reaches first and it holds two frame pipelines; the
         config domain holds no device, no pool and no dataplane ring. So the
         domain that PARSES an operator's — or an attacker's — XML must be the
         config domain, and the management domain's whole part is to copy bytes
         from a TCP connection into `cfg_request` and hand on. It never parses
         them. A parser in the management domain would put the reader of a hostile
         document in the same domain as a frame buffer and leave this split with
         nothing to withhold.

         AND WHY MANAGEMENT MAY NOT WRITE `cfg_reply`. That region carries the
         document `GET /config` answers with. A management domain that could write
         it could serve an operator a configuration the appliance is not running —
         a fabricated statement about the policy in force, which is worse than a
         wrong answer because an operator would edit and resubmit it. The read
         grant it does hold lets it copy that document to a client and nothing
         else.

         Sizes are `wire::CONFIG_REQUEST_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::CONFIG_REPLY_REGION_SIZE`, on the same footing as every constant
         above and held to them the same way: `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule for
         each and compares it. Both are 64 KiB of document plus a header, so
         seventeen pages each; they are placed at 0x3_100_000 and 0x3_200_000,
         clear of the eight-page `cfg` window this file's address-map block
         describes, so neither can grow into it.

         Neither carries phys_addr, for cfg's reason exactly: no device is told
         where they are, so an unplaced region — which Microkit allocates from
         general-purpose untyped memory, and which seL4 therefore zeroes — is the
         stronger position. -->
    <memory_region name="cfg_request" size="0x11000" />
    <memory_region name="cfg_reply" size="0x11000" />

    <!-- THE MANAGEMENT CHANNEL'S OWN SUBMISSION CHANNEL: the same ABI, a second
         instance, between the domain that terminates the channel and the domain
         that decides about a document.

         WHY A SECOND PAIR AND NOT THE ONE ABOVE. A submission channel admits ONE
         requester — its sequence number is the whole correlation, and a second
         writer would answer under numbers the first has outstanding — so two
         requesting domains are two channels by construction rather than by
         preference. And the two requesters are two domains at different points in
         the trust order: the domain that owns the management PORT is not the
         domain that terminates the management CHANNEL, and a region pair either
         could write would let each answer for the other. The CONFIG domain maps
         `chan_cfg_request` READ-ONLY and `chan_cfg_reply` READ-WRITE; the CRYPTO
         domain maps them the other way round, exactly as MANAGEMENT holds the pair
         above.

         WHAT THE CRYPTO DOMAIN GAINS, STATED PLAINLY: the ability to submit a
         configuration document and to ask that a commit be made, confirmed or put
         back. That is the authority a management plane has by definition, and this
         is the domain a management server's session terminates in — so it is the
         one place in this system where that authority can arrive at all. What it
         does NOT gain is any part of deciding: the reply region is the config
         domain's to write, so this domain cannot tell itself that a document was
         accepted, and it maps no part of `cfg`, the handover region the forwarding
         domain actually reads.

         AND WHAT THE CONFIG DOMAIN GAINS: nothing but a second question to answer.
         It maps one more region read-only and one more read-write, holds no new
         device, no pool, no ring and no network region, and its whole reach is
         still the handover it publishes and the two channels it answers.

         WHY THE DOCUMENT DOES NOT TRAVEL THE RELAY. The cryptography domain
         already speaks to the management domain over `relay_request`/`relay_reply`,
         and routing a staged document through them was considered and rejected:
         one relay item carries `wire::MAX_RELAY_PAYLOAD` and a document is four
         times that, so a document would have to be split across items and
         reassembled — which is a second framing layer, inside the domain that
         holds two frame pipelines, over bytes a management server chose. A region
         pair the deciding domain reads directly costs one more mapping in each of
         two domains and adds no parser anywhere.

         Sizes and placement are `cfg_request`'s: seventeen pages each, at
         0x3_300_000 and 0x3_400_000, clear of that pair's windows so neither can
         grow into the other. Neither carries phys_addr, for the same reason. -->
    <memory_region name="chan_cfg_request" size="0x11000" />
    <memory_region name="chan_cfg_reply" size="0x11000" />

    <!-- THE CONNECTION TABLE, and by two orders of magnitude the largest region
         in this system: 68 MiB and one page, `pd_runtime::FLOW_TABLE_REGION_SIZE`
         — a million entries of one cache line each, plus a bucket head per
         entry and a handful of cursors, rounded up to the mapping granularity.
         `lfw_flow::FLOW_CAPACITY` is the single knob it follows from, and
         changing it changes how many connections the appliance can carry at
         once.

         IT IS MAPPED BY EXACTLY ONE DOMAIN, READ-WRITE, and that exclusivity is
         not a convenience — it is what makes the borrow sound. Every other
         region here is shared, so a domain takes a `&` to a type whose fields
         are atomics or sit behind an unsafe accessor; this one is an ordinary
         Rust value with methods taking `&mut self`, and the forwarder takes a
         `&mut` to it. A second mapper would make that undefined rather than
         merely racy, which is why `xtask::sysdesc`'s rule for this region names
         one grant and quotes what every other domain's absence buys.

         No phys_addr, deliberately and for the reason `cfg` and `cfgack` have
         none: no device DMAs into it, so no driver needs to be told where it is,
         and an unplaced region is one Microkit allocates from general-purpose
         untyped memory — which is precisely the retyping seL4 ZEROES. That
         zeroing is load-bearing here in a way it is not for the ring regions: a
         table holds `FlowState`, a `#[repr(u8)]` enum whose `Vacant`
         discriminant is 0, so an all-zero region is a valid — if unlinked —
         table and *forming* the reference over it is defined. Over arbitrary
         bytes it would not be. This region therefore does not rest on the
         RAM-membership argument the address map above makes for the DMA
         regions, which nothing checks; it rests on Microkit not placing it.

         The forwarder calls `initialise` on it once at bring-up, which turns
         that valid-but-unlinked table into a usable one. Nothing persists across
         a restart of the domain, and that is the right side of the trade for a
         firewall: connections do not survive a restart of the thing deciding
         about them. -->
    <memory_region name="flow_table" size="0x4401000" />

    <!-- THE CLOCK CALIBRATION, and the first region in this system published
         under a SEQLOCK rather than under an identifying generation.

         It carries three words — the timestamp counter's measured frequency, the
         reading it was anchored on, and the wall-clock instant that reading
         corresponds to — and they are only meaningful TOGETHER: a frequency from
         one measurement anchored to another measurement's epoch names an instant
         that never existed. Every bit pattern of each of the three is
         individually plausible, so a torn triple is undetectable by inspection,
         which is exactly what `cfg`'s "publish the bytes, then release the
         generation" pattern relies on not being the case. `wire::ClockCalibration`
         therefore spends its counter on the write rather than on the identity —
         even settled, odd being written — and a reader that cannot get a matching
         pair of even readings answers "nothing" rather than assembling a triple
         from two publishes.

         ONE WRITER AND TEN READERS, and the direction is the whole grant. The
         clock domain maps it READ-WRITE: it is the domain that measured the
         numbers, and it publishes them once, after the record that says what it
         measured. Every other domain maps it READ-ONLY, on the same footing as
         `cfg`: each is a CONSUMER of what was measured, and a domain that could
         write it would be able to move this node's own idea of time — every
         retransmission deadline and every reaping deadline of the transport on
         the management port, every instant a log record is dated with, and one
         day every certificate's validity window — from a domain
         that answers an attacker.

         THE READ GRANT WIDENED — a reviewed capability change — when every record gained the
         instant it was emitted at. Six domains were added: the forwarder, the
         configuration domain, the three drivers and the console. Each stamps its
         own records, so each needs the three words that turn a counter reading
         into a time, and a domain that could not read them would emit a
         transcript no reader could place in time. The recorder is the eighth
         reader and arrived on the same terms: it stamps its own records, and it
         will one day date every observation it commits to the medium — a capture
         whose timestamps are the appliance's own idea of time and not a
         separate one. The hardware probe is the ninth reader and the
         cryptography domain the tenth, on the same terms again: each stamps
         its own records and reads nothing else here.

         WHAT THE ADDED GRANT GIVES, exactly: a READ of three integers, and
         nothing else. It confers no ability to write the calibration, no channel
         to the clock domain in either direction, no wakeup capability on it, and
         no reach into any other region — the page carries a frequency, an anchor
         reading and an epoch, and holding it lets a domain answer "what time is
         it" and no other question. A compromised domain gains a number it could
         already have approximated from its own `RDTSC`, which every domain may
         execute in any case: seL4 leaves `CR4.TSD` clear, so the counter is not
         what the grant confers either. The console in particular still cannot
         rewrite the frequency it is printing, which was the exclusion this
         region's grant was drawn around, and the direction is what preserves it.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD: the write, from every domain but the one that
         measured, which is the whole of the asymmetry. Nothing else in this
         system maps the region at all.

         WHY THE MANAGEMENT DOMAIN READS A REGION RATHER THAN ASKING. There is no
         IPC for a timestamp: that domain reads `RDTSC` itself, which is one
         unprivileged instruction, and converts it with the triple published here.
         A channel to the clock domain would put a round trip on the path of every
         frame and would give this domain a wakeup capability on a domain that
         runs once and parks. What that costs is stated at pds/management: with no
         channel, a republished calibration is picked up on the next frame.

         Size is `wire::CLOCK_CALIBRATION_REGION_SIZE`, its type's `size_of`
         rounded up to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every
         constant above and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a
         rule for this region and compares it. `ClockCalibration` is 32 bytes, so
         it rounds to one page.

         No phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above gives for `cfg`:
         no device DMAs into it, so no domain needs its physical address, and an
         unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose untyped memory — which
         is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes, so `attach_region!`'s
         zero-initialisation precondition holds by construction. A zeroed region
         is generation zero, which `load` refuses, so a management domain that
         comes up before the clock domain has published reads "no calibration"
         rather than a triple of zeroes. -->
    <memory_region name="clock" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- WHETHER THIS APPLIANCE HAS AN OWNER: one word, one writer, one reader.

         The store domain holds the appliance's identity and is the only thing
         that can change whether a management plane has taken it — by minting on
         a fresh medium, by reading the record back on a later boot, or by
         committing an onboarding package. It maps this read-write. The forwarder
         maps it read-only and refuses every frame while the word is not the one
         that means owned, so an appliance nobody has onboarded forwards nothing.

         WHY THE FORWARDER IS THE READER AND NOT THE CONFIGURATION DOMAIN. The
         refusal is a dataplane verdict — counted per reason, recorded in the
         capture, named on the console — so it belongs where every other verdict
         is reached. Routing it through a configuration generation would make
         "unowned" a table the configuration domain composes, which would put the
         appliance's ownership behind the parser that reads an attacker's
         document.

         NO CHANNEL, and that is the whole reason this costs one region and no
         notification. The forwarder is woken by every frame that arrives, so it
         reads this word on a wakeup it was going to have; a notify direction from
         the store domain to the forwarder would be a capability on the dataplane
         granted to the domain that holds the private key, bought for a latency
         nothing measures.

         ONE WORD AND NO SEQLOCK, unlike `clock` above: a naturally aligned `u32`
         store is one access, so there is no torn read for a counter to bracket.
         Size is `wire::OWNERSHIP_REGION_SIZE`, its type's `size_of` rounded up to
         the mapping granularity and held to it by `xtask::sysdesc` like every
         constant here; four bytes round to one page, which is what a region costs
         when a region is the unit of grant.

         No phys_addr, on `clock`'s reasoning exactly — and the zeroing that
         follows from it is load-bearing here rather than merely sound: a zeroed
         word is not the owned token, so a forwarder that runs before the store
         domain has published anything forwards nothing. -->
    <memory_region name="owner" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- WHERE THIS APPLIANCE DIALS ITS MANAGEMENT PLANE: one word, one writer,
         one reader, and the same shape as `owner` above for the same reasons.

         The store domain holds the state record an onboarding package wrote and
         is the only thing that can change where this appliance answers to — by
         minting on a fresh medium, by reading the record back on a later boot,
         or by committing a package. It maps this read-write. The management
         domain maps it read-only and will open the outbound channel to whatever
         is published here; while nothing is, it dials nowhere.

         WHY THE MANAGEMENT DOMAIN IS THE READER AND THE OTHER TWO ARE NOT. It is
         the domain that will hold the session, so the address belongs where the
         socket is opened. The read grant is withheld from the CONFIGURATION
         domain, so where this appliance reports to cannot become a value the
         parser that reads an attacker's document composes — an appliance whose
         management address could be moved by a document would be one an
         administrator could redirect by uploading a file. And it is withheld from
         the FORWARDER, which decides frames: a dataplane that knew the management
         address could treat that traffic specially, and a compromised one could
         tell an operator's session from everything else it carries. Neither
         domain has any use for the address, and a grant nobody needs is one that
         outlives its reason.

         WHAT A READ GRANT HERE CONFERS, stated plainly because it is the whole of
         the exposure: an address literal and a port, both of which the management
         server publishes to every appliance it owns and which appear in the
         clear on the wire of any session opened to it. It is not a credential and
         it authenticates nothing — what makes a session to that address safe is
         the anchor the cryptography domain holds and the certificate the peer
         presents under it, neither of which crosses here.

         TWO READERS, AND THE SECOND IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE ROW CHANGED. The
         management domain reads it to know where to dial. The CRYPTOGRAPHY
         domain reads it because the address is half of the trust decision the
         channel makes: a management server's certificate is validated against
         the ADDRESS LITERAL that was dialled and against no name, so the domain
         that validates reads the address itself rather than being handed one by
         the domain that owns the network — which could otherwise point that
         validation at a name a server it controls holds a certificate for. What
         a read confers either way is an address and a port that every appliance
         the management plane owns is told and that appear in the clear on the
         wire, so the grant is narrow because what it carries authenticates
         nothing. The WRITE grant is still the store domain's alone, and is
         withheld from both readers.

         NO CHANNEL, in either direction, on `owner`'s reasoning. The management
         domain is woken on a period by the clock domain and by the traffic on its
         own port, so it reads this word on a wakeup it was going to have; a
         notify direction from the domain that holds the private key into it would
         buy a latency nothing measures.

         ONE WORD AND NO SEQLOCK, and here that is load-bearing rather than merely
         cheap: an address and a port are meaningful only together, so a torn pair
         could spell a destination nobody published. They share one naturally
         aligned 64-bit word — 32 bits of address, 16 of port, 16 of tag — which
         is one access, so there is no torn read for a counter to bracket. Size is
         `wire::ENDPOINT_REGION_SIZE`, its type's `size_of` rounded up to the
         mapping granularity and held to it by `xtask::sysdesc` like every
         constant here.

         No phys_addr, on `owner`'s reasoning exactly — and the zeroing that
         follows from it is load-bearing here too: a zeroed word carries no tag, a
         zero port and an unspecified address, so a reader that runs before the
         store domain has published anything finds nowhere to dial rather than
         somewhere. -->
    <memory_region name="endpoint" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE CAPTURE TAP, one region per direction, and the third handover in
         this system built the way the log ring and the configuration handover
         are.

         `tap` carries what the forwarder has to say about the frames it decided
         on, and `tap_consume` carries how far the recorder has read. Two regions
         rather than one for the reason `cfg`/`cfgack` are two and each log ring
         is two: a region is the unit of grant, so only two can give each domain
         write access to exactly the direction it speaks in.

         THE PERMS ARE THE WHOLE ARGUMENT, and they are the log ring's, mirrored
         onto a different pair of domains. The FORWARDER maps `tap` READ-WRITE
         and `tap_consume` READ-ONLY: it is the producer, and a producer that
         could move the consume cursor would reuse slots the recorder had not
         read — discarding observations while its own drop count stayed at zero,
         which is silent loss reported as none. The RECORDER maps `tap`
         READ-ONLY and `tap_consume` READ-WRITE: how far it has read is its own
         statement about itself, and a recorder that could write the ring could
         invent an observation the forwarder never made and commit it to the
         medium as that domain's testimony. A capture an operator reads as
         evidence must not be forgeable by the domain that stores it.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD: every other domain, in either direction. No driver
         maps it, so a compromised driver cannot see what was decided about the
         frames it delivered; the management domain does not, so the domain that
         answers a download cannot read the tap ahead of the recorder or write
         into it; and the console does not, which keeps the two output channels
         disjoint.

         WHAT A SLOT HOLDS is an annotation and a bounded copy of the frame. The
         annotation is the appliance's decision — the verdict and its reason, the
         flow the frame belongs to as a slot-and-generation pair with its
         classification and state, the rule that decided it, the lifecycle or
         policy event it caused, an interface index, two lengths and an instant.
         Behind it sit up to `wire::TAP_SNAP_LEN` bytes of the frame as it
         arrived, which is what a recording is *for* and why the payload
         exception the design confines to the two recording sinks reaches this
         region as well. It is still not a pool: the copy is the forwarder's own
         and the recorder gets no descriptor, no buffer index and no way to reach
         a frame in flight, so nothing here lets the recorder touch traffic the
         appliance is still forwarding. `wire::tap` is where the record's shape
         and its checking live.

         Sizes are `wire::TAP_RECORDS_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::TAP_CONSUME_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded up to
         the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant above and
         held to it the same way. `TapRecords` is sixty-four slots of an
         eighty-byte annotation and a two-kilobyte frame copy, plus its two
         cursors, so it rounds to thirty-four pages; `TapConsume` is one word
         and costs the page a region costs, exactly as `cfgack` does.

         Neither carries phys_addr: no device DMAs into either, so no domain
         needs their physical address, and an unplaced region is allocated from
         general-purpose untyped memory — precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes, so
         `attach_region!`'s zero-initialisation precondition holds by
         construction and a zeroed pair is a valid empty ring. -->
    <memory_region name="tap" size="0x22000" />
    <memory_region name="tap_consume" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE DOWNLOAD HANDOVER, one region per direction, and it runs the
         opposite way round from the tap above.

         `dl_request` carries what the management domain has been asked for — a
         recording, an offset, a length — and `dl_reply` carries the window of
         bytes the recorder answered with. The MANAGEMENT domain maps
         `dl_request` READ-WRITE and `dl_reply` READ-ONLY; the RECORDER maps
         `dl_request` READ-ONLY and `dl_reply` READ-WRITE. Each domain writes
         exactly the direction it speaks in, and neither can forge the other's
         half: a management domain that could write the reply could serve an
         operator bytes no medium holds, and a recorder that could write the
         request could answer a question nobody asked.

         WHY THE REPLY IS A COPY AND NOT A POINTER. The obvious design hands the
         management domain a window into `blk_io` and saves the copy. It is
         refused: that region is where everything the recorder is currently
         staging lives, download or not, so a read grant on it would let the
         domain an attacker reaches first read whatever the recorder happened to
         have in hand. A bounded region the recorder fills deliberately is the
         narrower grant, and 36 KiB is what it costs.

         THE FORWARDER MAPS NEITHER, in either direction, and that is the
         counterpart of it mapping no `blk_io`: the dataplane cannot see what an
         operator is downloading and cannot influence it. Nor may it — a
         forwarder able to write `dl_reply` would be able to answer a download
         with bytes of its own while the recorder reported having served the
         medium's.

         Sizes are `wire::DOWNLOAD_REQUEST_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::DOWNLOAD_REPLY_REGION_SIZE`, on the same footing as every
         constant above. `DownloadRequest` fits a page; `DownloadReply` is
         `DOWNLOAD_WINDOW_LEN` bytes of window plus its header, and rounds to
         nine pages.

         Neither carries phys_addr, for the reason `cfg` does not. -->
    <memory_region name="dl_request" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="dl_reply" size="0x9000" />

    <!-- THE SIGNING DELEGATION, one region per direction, and the ONE handover in
         this description whose whole purpose is that something CANNOT cross it.

         `sign_request` carries what the cryptography domain is asking for — sign
         these bytes, or tell me which key you hold — and `sign_reply` carries the
         answer. The CRYPTOGRAPHY domain maps `sign_request` READ-WRITE and
         `sign_reply` READ-ONLY; the STORE domain maps `sign_request` READ-ONLY and
         `sign_reply` READ-WRITE. Each writes exactly the direction it speaks in,
         which is `dl_request`/`dl_reply`'s split with the roles exchanged.

         WHY THE ASYMMETRY IS LOAD-BEARING HERE AND NOT MERELY TIDY. A domain that
         could write the reply could publish a signature the device key never
         produced and then present it as the appliance's — a forged authentication
         rather than a wrong answer. So the domain that authenticates cannot write
         the region it reads its own signatures out of, and the domain that holds
         the key cannot write the region that states the question.

         THE KEY CANNOT CROSS, AND THAT NEEDS BOTH HALVES OF THIS ROW. `wire`'s
         `SignRequest` and `SignReply` have NO FIELD FOR A PRIVATE KEY in either
         direction: there is a message, a signature, a public point, a device name,
         a status and a sequence, and nothing a 32-byte scalar has a place in. That
         is the ABI half, and it would not be enough on its own — an ABI that could
         carry a key would carry it through perfectly correct grants. The grants are
         the other half: the store domain is the only writer of `sign_reply`, so the
         only bytes the cryptography domain can read are ones that domain chose to
         publish, and it publishes what the ABI has fields for. Neither property
         alone establishes it; together they do, and the sysdesc checker holds the
         grants while the type system holds the fields.

         EVERY OTHER DOMAIN MAPS NEITHER, in either direction — no driver, no
         forwarder, no recorder, no management domain, no console. The forwarder in
         particular: a dataplane domain able to write `sign_request` could have the
         appliance's key sign bytes of its own choosing, which is the one thing a
         signing oracle must not be. The management domain is excluded too, and that
         is deliberate rather than pending: when it terminates sessions it will ask
         over a channel of its own, granted then and reviewed then.

         Sizes are `wire::SIGN_REQUEST_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::SIGN_REPLY_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded up to the
         mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant above. Both fit
         one page: a request is a 256-byte message and four words, and a reply is a
         72-byte signature, a 65-byte point, a 16-byte name and five words.

         Neither carries phys_addr, for the reason `cfg` does not. -->
    <memory_region name="sign_request" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="sign_reply" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE ONBOARDING PACKAGE'S STAGING REGION, and the one region in this
         description whose whole content is a stranger's bytes.

         An administrator uploads a tar archive to the onboarding port. The
         CRYPTOGRAPHY domain terminates that session and accumulates the upload
         into this region, which it maps READ-WRITE; the STORE domain — which
         holds the device key and owns the medium the ownership is written to —
         maps it READ-ONLY and reads it to decide whether to install. Nobody else
         maps it in either direction.

         WHY A REGION AND NOT THE DELEGATION'S MESSAGE FIELD. `sign_request`
         carries 256 bytes and an archive is bounded at 128 KiB, so chunking one
         through it would be five hundred and twelve attacker-paced round trips
         and a reassembler holding partial state in the domain that owns the
         medium. That would cost the delegation the one property worth keeping —
         one demand produces one reply — and would hold it only as long as the
         peer cooperated. Thirty-two pages of address space buys the whole
         archive present or absent, and the request states only how many bytes of
         it there are.

         THE DIRECTION IS THE UNCOMFORTABLE PART AND IS THE POINT. This region is
         written by the domain an UNAUTHENTICATED PEER talks to and read by the
         domain that holds the appliance's private key, which is the only such
         edge in this system. What makes it safe is not the grant and not the
         writer's good behaviour: it is that the reader takes a SNAPSHOT into its
         own `blk2_io` window before it looks at a byte, so every rule is applied
         to bytes the writer can no longer reach, and the archive that passes is
         byte-for-byte the archive that is written to a sector. The grant's half
         of it is the perms — the store domain cannot write here, so it cannot
         install an archive nobody uploaded, and the cryptography domain cannot
         read the reply into existence.

         WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT REACH. The store domain gains no path to a frame:
         this region carries no address, no descriptor and no pointer, and the
         domain that fills it holds no network region of its own either. What
         crosses is a byte string, and a byte string is the one thing a parser
         with no allocator, bounded loops and typed refusals is built for.

         The size is `wire::INSTALL_STAGING_REGION_SIZE`, its type's `size_of`
         rounded up to the mapping granularity and equal to it exactly: the
         archive bound is already a whole number of pages, so a larger region
         would be address space granted for nothing.

         It carries no phys_addr, for the reason `cfg` does not. -->
    <memory_region name="install_staging" size="0x20000" />

    <!-- THE TLS RELAY, one region per direction, and the handover that lets the
         appliance terminate an encrypted session without the domain that owns the
         network holding a key, a session secret or a plaintext byte.

         `relay_request` carries what the MANAGEMENT domain has: a connection was
         accepted on the onboarding port, here are the bytes that arrived, is
         there anything to send, the connection ended. `relay_reply` carries what
         the CRYPTOGRAPHY domain answers with: the bytes to put on the wire, and
         whether the session is over. The management domain maps `relay_request`
         READ-WRITE and `relay_reply` READ-ONLY; the cryptography domain maps the
         reverse. Each writes exactly the direction it speaks in, which is
         `sign_request`/`sign_reply`'s split with a different pair of domains.

         WHAT CROSSES IS CIPHERTEXT THE MANAGEMENT DOMAIN ALREADY CARRIED. Both
         directions hold TLS records: one direction is what a peer put on the
         wire and the management domain read off it, the other is what the
         management domain is about to put back. So this pair widens that
         domain's reach over nothing — it already had those bytes, or is about to.
         What it does NOT gain is everything behind them: the arena, the device
         private key, the handshake secrets and every decrypted byte stay in the
         cryptography domain, and no field of either region has a place for one.

         WHY THE ASYMMETRY IS LOAD-BEARING HERE AND NOT MERELY TIDY. A domain that
         could write the reply could put bytes of its own choosing on the wire as
         though the terminating end had produced them — under a session
         authenticated by the appliance's own identity, that is a forgery rather
         than a wrong answer. So the domain that owns the network cannot write the
         region it reads its records out of, and the domain that terminates the
         session cannot write the region that states what arrived.

         ONE CONNECTION, AND THAT IS THE ABI RATHER THAN A RULE. `wire::relay`
         carries NO CONNECTION IDENTIFIER in either direction — no handle, no
         index, no generation. Every operation names *the* connection and
         `RelayOperation::Open` is the only thing that begins one, so a second
         concurrent connection would need a second name and there is nowhere to
         put one. A management domain that is wrong, confused or compromised
         cannot ask this pair to carry two. Nor can it ask for a plaintext: the
         operation vocabulary has four values and none of them means "give me what
         the peer said".

         EVERY OTHER DOMAIN MAPS NEITHER, in either direction — no driver, no
         forwarder, no recorder, no console, no configuration domain and NOT THE
         STORE DOMAIN. That last is the one worth stating: the domain that holds
         the private scalar maps no region a frame's bytes reach, so the path from
         the onboarding port to the key is still two hops through an ABI with no
         field for one.

         Sizes are `wire::RELAY_REQUEST_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::RELAY_REPLY_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded up to
         the mapping granularity. Five pages each, because `wire::MAX_RELAY_PAYLOAD`
         is one maximal TLS record — a five-byte header, 2^14 of content and 256
         of expansion — so a record crosses whole and neither end holds a partial
         one while the other is asked for the rest.

         Neither carries phys_addr, for the reason `cfg` does not. -->
    <memory_region name="relay_request" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="relay_reply" size="0x5000" />

    <!-- The log transport: one ring per WRITING domain, and TWO REGIONS PER
         RING, one for each direction.

         The console is a device with one owner (see the console domain below),
         so every other domain reaches it by publishing a typed record into a
         ring of its own and letting that domain render it. One ring per writer
         rather than one shared ring is the same argument the pipeline and the
         configuration handover make, sharpened by what a single writer buys
         here: the ring is single-producer/single-consumer, so one writer per
         ring makes a torn record — two domains' bytes interleaved into one
         console line — unrepresentable rather than prevented by scheduling. It
         is also the only shape the ring protocol admits at all; a second
         producer on one of these is two writers racing for the same slot.

         THE SPLIT EXISTS SO THE TWO DIRECTIONS CARRY OPPOSITE PERMISSIONS, and
         that is the whole reason there are sixteen rows here rather than eight. A
         region is the unit of grant, so only two regions can give each domain
         write access to exactly the direction it speaks in — the same reason
         `cfg`/`cfgack` are two regions and each dataplane pipeline is three.

         `log_<domain>` carries the slots, the producer cursor and the writer's
         drop count. The writing domain maps it READ-WRITE and the console maps
         it READ-ONLY, so THE CONSOLE CANNOT FORGE A RECORD INTO A DOMAIN'S
         RING: it cannot store into a slot, cannot advance the cursor that
         publishes one, and cannot rewrite the count of what that domain says it
         lost. A console that could would be able to attribute a line to a
         domain that never emitted it — and it is the one domain in the system
         whose output an operator reads as testimony about the others.

         `log_<domain>_consume` carries the console's consume cursor and nothing
         else. The console maps it READ-WRITE and the writing domain maps it
         READ-ONLY, so A WRITER CANNOT FORGE THE CURSOR THAT SAYS HOW MUCH OF
         ITS RING HAS BEEN READ. A writer that could would move it forward to
         reuse slots the console has not rendered, discarding records while its
         own drop count stayed at zero — silent loss reported as none, which is
         worse than loss.

         A single region could carry neither property. `wire`'s
         `LogReader::read` publishes its consume position on every record it
         takes (crates/wire/src/log_ring.rs), and that word is the writer's only
         flow control: mapped read-only the console would fault on the first
         record it consumed, and mapped read-write it would hold write access to
         every slot in the ring. Two regions is what removes the choice between
         those two.

         What the rows isolate BETWEEN writers is unchanged and still worth
         stating: no writer maps another writer's region in either direction, so
         a compromised parser domain cannot read what a driver has said about
         itself, cannot rewrite it, and cannot silence it. Every pair of writing
         domains is disjoint here, and that is enforced by these rows and by
         `xtask::sysdesc`'s per-mapper grant set.

         The residue, named rather than left to be discovered: the console still
         decides what reaches the line, so it can decline to print any record
         and can emit any line it likes as itself — it owns the port and is the
         sole renderer. What it can no longer do is put words in another
         domain's mouth, or make that domain lose records it will report as
         delivered.

         Sizes are `wire::LOG_RECORDS_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::LOG_CONSUME_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded up
         to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant above
         and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule per
         region and compares it. `LogRecords` is 13320 bytes (64 slots of a
         208-byte record, plus the producer cursor and the drop count) so each
         is four pages; `LogConsume` is one word and so costs the page a region
         costs, exactly as `cfgack` does. The twenty together are 200 KiB.

         None carries phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above
         gives for `cfg` and `cfgack`: no device DMAs into a log region, so no
         domain needs its physical address, so the tool has no reason to demand
         a fixed one — and an unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose
         untyped memory, which is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes. That is
         what makes `attach_region!`'s zero-initialisation precondition hold by
         construction here, and it is also what makes the boot transcript work:
         a zeroed pair is a valid empty ring, so a domain may publish records
         during its own `init`, before the console domain has run at all, and
         they are still there when it comes up. -->
    <memory_region name="log_forwarder" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_forwarder_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver0" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver0_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver1" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver1_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_config" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_config_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_clock" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_clock_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver2" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver2_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_management" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_management_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_recorder" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_recorder_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_hardware_probe" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_hardware_probe_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_crypto" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_crypto_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_store" size="0x5000" />
    <memory_region name="log_store_consume" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE TRANSCRIPT RELAY: the one pair of log regions that runs the other
         way, from the console domain to the domain that writes the medium.

         WHY IT EXISTS. The management server stores this appliance's log, and
         those lines have to reach the recording medium. The recorder is the only
         domain that can write that medium and it maps NO log ring but its own —
         widening that would delete the property the twenty-two rows above are
         granted for, and it would hand eleven read grants to the domain holding a
         block device and a DMA-capable controller. The console domain is the
         opposite: it already maps all eleven read-only and already renders every
         record. So the transcript travels the way the authority already runs. The
         console publishes each line it has just printed and the recorder copies
         it out. One pair of pages instead of eleven read grants, and the recorder
         learns what other domains have said rather than gaining the ability to
         read whatever a peer happens to be writing.

         WHAT EACH SIDE MAY DO, and it is the log rings' split read in the mirror.
         `log_relay` holds the line slots, the producer cursor and the console's
         drop count: the console maps it READ-WRITE and the recorder READ-ONLY, so
         a compromised recorder can decline to frame a line and cannot forge one
         into the recording a management server believes. `log_relay_consume`
         holds the consumer cursor alone: the recorder maps it read-write and the
         console read-only, so the console cannot forge how far the recorder has
         read and so cannot overwrite a line that has not been framed while
         counting no drop for it. One region could not carry both authorities,
         exactly as `cfg`/`cfgack` cannot.

         A FULL RELAY COSTS A COUNTED DROP AND NEVER THE CONSOLE. The console is
         this appliance's only diagnostic surface, and one that waited on the
         recorder would go quiet exactly when the recorder is what is wrong. So
         the console's publish is total and non-blocking: the serial line gets
         every line whatever this region's state, and a relay the recorder is not
         draining fast enough shows up as
         `librefirewall_console_transcript_lines_total{outcome="dropped"}` — a gap
         in the recorded transcript, never a stalled console.

         WHAT IS NOT WIDENED. The recorder still maps no log ring but its own, in
         either direction, and no third domain maps either of these regions. What
         crosses is a line the console has already printed, not a record a peer
         wrote, so a compromised console can put words in the recording that it
         could equally have put on the serial port — which it can do anyway,
         owning the port and being the sole renderer. The forwarder maps neither
         side, exactly as it maps no log ring but its own.

         Sizes are `wire::LOG_RELAY_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::LOG_RELAY_CONSUME_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded
         up to the mapping granularity and held to it by `xtask::sysdesc`.
         `LogRelay` is deliberately ONE page — a second page of authority on the
         domain holding the block device is what this whole region avoids — and it
         carries every slot that fits behind its header, which is fifteen lines of
         up to 256 bytes. `LogRelayConsume` is one word and costs the page a
         region costs.

         Neither carries phys_addr, for the log rings' reason: no device DMAs into
         one, and a zeroed pair is a valid empty relay, so the console may publish
         before the recorder has run. -->
    <memory_region name="log_relay" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_relay_consume" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE ARENA: the appliance's only allocator, and the only region in this
         system with no structure.

         WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. A proven TLS implementation requires an
         allocator, and every dataplane domain still has none — this is the one
         exception and it is confined to the one domain that runs TLS. What
         makes an allocator on a path an adversary drives acceptable is stated
         as a mechanism rather than an intention: it is BOUNDED by this
         region's size, its exhaustion is a typed refusal that closes the
         session, and the domain reports its high-water mark against this
         capacity on every boot so the bound is a number an operator can see
         rather than one the code asserts.

         NO phys_addr, DELIBERATELY. Nothing DMAs into it, so no driver needs
         its physical address and the Microkit tool has no reason to demand a
         fixed one — which means it is allocated from general-purpose untyped
         memory, is kernel-zeroed, and costs NOTHING from the physical address
         window the packed range above rations. The store device's regions will
         come out of that window; this one does not.

         WHY TWO MEGABYTES. Far above what a session measures — the domain
         reports the figure — and small enough to be an ordinary mapping. It is
         a whole number of pages, which is what makes an offset the allocator
         aligns an address that is aligned.

         WHO MAPS IT: the cryptography domain, read-write, and nobody else. It
         holds the ephemeral keys of a TLS session while one is running, so a
         second mapper would be a second reader of key material. -->
    <memory_region name="arena_crypto" size="0x400000" />


    <!-- THE METRIC SHARDS: one region per protection domain, each written by
         that domain alone and read by the management domain alone.

         WHY TWELVE REGIONS AND NOT ONE. A region is the unit of grant, and the
         property worth having here is that a domain's numbers are *its own
         testimony*: the management domain composes every one of them into the
         reading a recording carries upstream, so it must read all twelve, and a
         single shared region would make it a writer of all twelve too. It could then
         attribute a driver's dropped frames to another driver, or zero a count
         that accuses itself — and it is the domain that faces the
         management-plane attacker. Twelve regions with twelve writers is what
         makes each series a claim only its own domain can make, and it is the
         same argument the twenty-two log regions make one step further: there the
         console cannot forge a *record*, here it cannot forge a *number*.

         PER-DOMAIN SERIES, NO TOTALS. Nothing in this node sums across shards.
         A summed total is corrupted by a domain restart — one shard goes back to
         zero, the sum goes backwards, and a consumer differencing two readings
         forges an enormous rate — whereas a `domain=`-labelled series resets
         alone, which a time-series database handles by design. This row set is what
         makes that no-total decision possible.

         SINGLE WRITER IS THE WHOLE SYNCHRONISATION. Exactly one domain has each
         region read-write, so an increment is a relaxed 64-bit load, an add and
         a relaxed store — no `lock` prefix and no contention — and on x86_64 an
         aligned 64-bit access cannot tear, so the reader never sees half a
         value. Unlike `clock` this needs no seqlock: a calibration's three words
         are meaningful only together and a counter is a number in its own right
         (`lfw_metrics`, crate header).

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD: every domain but the writer and the reader, in
         either direction. A driver maps no other driver's shard, the forwarder
         maps none of them but its own, and the console — which renders an
         operator's only other output — maps none at all. A domain that could
         write another's shard could make a port that is dropping every frame
         report a clean line.

         AND THE READER CANNOT WRITE. Every grant to `management` below is
         READ-ONLY except its own. That is the same asymmetry `cfg` and `clock`
         have, and for a sharper reason: this domain is the one an attacker
         reaches first, and a set of numbers it could edit would let a compromise
         of it hide the compromise in everything it reports upstream.

         `stats_management` HAS EXACTLY ONE MAPPER, and that is not an oversight.
         The management domain both writes and reads it, because the walk that
         composes a reading crosses one uniform array of twelve shards rather than
         eleven regions plus a live read of its own counters — one code path, and
         a set of numbers taken at one publish rather than half of them changing
         under the walk.
         It costs no cross-domain authority: no other domain maps it.

         Size is `lfw_metrics::STATS_REGION_SIZE`, the type's `size_of` rounded
         up to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant
         above and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule per
         region and compares it. A `StatsShard` is 2688 bytes, so each rounds to
         one page and the twelve cost 48 KiB. It grew from 768 with the filter, and
         the page count did not: the shard is sized by the widest set a domain
         publishes, and the forwarder's now reserves one slot per rule the
         configuration ABI admits — which is why a second region for those
         counters would have bought back nothing and added a mapping here.

         None carries phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above gives
         for `cfg`: no device DMAs into a shard, so no domain needs its physical
         address, and an unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose
         untyped memory — precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes. A zeroed shard is
         every counter at zero, which is the state a domain that has done nothing
         is in, so a reading taken before a domain has published carries zeroes
         rather than rubbish. -->
    <memory_region name="stats_forwarder" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver0" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver1" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver2" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_management" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_console" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_config" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_clock" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_recorder" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_hardware_probe" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_crypto" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_store" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE STATISTICS RELAY, and it exists because the two halves of one job
         are in two domains that may not be merged.

         The recorder is the only domain that can write the medium, and it maps
         no statistics shard but its own. The management domain maps every
         shard and can therefore compose one whole reading, and it can write no
         recording. So a metric snapshot destined for a recording has to cross,
         and this page is the crossing: the MANAGEMENT domain maps it READ-WRITE
         and the RECORDER maps it READ-ONLY, which is `dl_request`'s direction
         with a different pair of domains.

         WHY THIS AND NOT ELEVEN MORE READ GRANTS ON THE RECORDER. Widening
         `stats_*` to the recorder would delete the property those grants exist
         for — one writer and one reader per shard — on the domain that holds a
         block device and a DMA-capable controller. This page is the narrower
         grant by every measure: one region instead of eleven, one direction
         instead of eleven, and the recorder learns numbers rather than gaining
         the ability to read whatever a peer is currently counting.

         WHAT THE RECORDER CANNOT DO WITH IT. It is READ-ONLY there, so a
         compromised recorder cannot forge a number into a reading or into
         another domain's shard: it can only decline to write what it
         was handed, which is a recording that says less and never one that lies.
         And the management domain, which writes this page, gains nothing on the
         medium — it cannot place a block, only offer numbers the recorder
         chooses to frame.

         WHY A SEQLOCK WHERE A SHARD NEEDS NONE. A shard's slots are
         independently meaningful; a *reading* is meaningful only whole, its
         whole claim being that every number in it was taken at one instant. So
         this region carries `clock`'s generation counter and its protocol — even
         settled, odd being written — and the reader takes a bounded number of
         attempts and then reports having missed one (`wire::StatsRelay`). The
         counter doubles as the pace: it rises once per publish, so the recorder
         writes a block when it has moved and never on a timer of its own.

         NO OTHER DOMAIN MAPS IT, in either direction. The forwarder in
         particular does not: the dataplane neither publishes nor observes this
         page, exactly as it maps no shard but its own.

         Size is `wire::STATS_RELAY_REGION_SIZE`, on the same footing as every
         constant above. The type is one page by construction — a header and as
         many slots as fit behind it — and `lfw_metrics` asserts at build time
         that the catalogue fits, so a reading that outgrew the page is a
         compile error rather than one silently cut short.

         No phys_addr, for the reason the shards above carry none: no device
         DMAs into it, and a zeroed region is generation zero, which every
         reader reads as "nothing published yet". -->
    <memory_region name="stats_relay" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- The forwarder maps four dataplane regions and two configuration ones,
         and holds no device capability at all. IT IS GRANTED NO MANAGEMENT
         REGION, in either direction and of either pipeline, and the management
         domain below is granted no dataplane region — the mutual exclusion
         required of a management port that carries no forwarded
         traffic. Neither half is a comment: `xtask::sysdesc` names the mapper
         set of every region exactly, so either grant appearing fails the gate at
         the point the edit is made.
         `RouteStage` decides on each frame and rewrites its Ethernet and IPv4
         headers in the buffer the receiving NIC DMA'd it into, so it is granted
         the two pools as well as the two ring pairs: a domain that rewrites a
         header must reach the bytes, and that authority is the price of routing
         in place rather than copying every frame between two pools.

         What stays withheld is the `free` ring of either pipeline, and it
         matters more than the pool ever did. A forged return puts a live DMA
         target back onto an owner's free stack, to be handed out a second time
         while the first NIC is still writing it; and the forwarder cannot
         produce onto that ring in any case without being a second producer on
         an SPSC ring that admits exactly one. Corrupting a frame in flight —
         which the pool grant does permit — damages one packet, inside a region
         both drivers already share.

         272 KiB mapped: the same byte count as the two undivided pipeline
         regions this replaced, and deliberately not the same bytes. What the
         split buys is no longer a smaller grant but a different one.

         It additionally maps the two configuration regions, and the perms are
         the whole of the argument. `cfg` is READ-ONLY here: the forwarder is
         the domain that decides under the configuration, so it must read it,
         and a domain that could also write it could rewrite the table it is
         about to be held to and leave the publishing domain reporting a
         generation nobody is running. `cfgack` is read-write because
         acknowledging is this domain's own statement about itself — what it has
         staged and what it is running — and it is the only domain that may make
         it. The two perms together are what make the handover a protocol
         between two domains rather than a shared scratch area.

         IT NOW ALSO PRODUCES ONTO THE CAPTURE TAP, and the perms are the log
         ring's argument on a different pair of domains. `tap` is
         read-write here because this domain is the producer: what it decided
         about a frame is its own testimony, and it is the only domain that may
         make it. `tap_consume` is READ-ONLY, so THIS DOMAIN CANNOT FORGE HOW
         MUCH THE RECORDER HAS READ — one that could would advance the cursor to
         reuse slots the recorder had not taken, discarding observations while
         its own drop count stayed at zero, which is a capture with holes in it
         that reports none.

         What the tap does NOT give this domain is any reach toward the medium.
         It maps no `blk_io`, no `blk_dma`, no `bar3` and no `ecam3`, and it maps
         neither download region, so a compromised forwarder can put annotations
         on a ring the recorder is free to refuse and can do nothing else: it
         cannot write a sector, cannot read what has been recorded, and cannot
         see or answer a download. -->
    <protection_domain name="forwarder" priority="2" stack_size="0x20000">
        <program_image path="forwarder.elf" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="fwd0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="fwd1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool0" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="pool0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool1" vaddr="0x2_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="pool1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="tap" vaddr="0x2_400_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tap_vaddr" />
        <map mr="tap_consume" vaddr="0x2_500_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tap_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfgack" vaddr="0x3_008_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfgack_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="owner" vaddr="0x3_00a_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="owner_vaddr" />
        <map mr="flow_table" vaddr="0x8_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="flow_table_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_forwarder" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The configuration domain: it reads the document compiled into it,
         refuses or commits it, and publishes the result. Its whole grant is six
         mappings and nothing else: the handover pair above, mirrored — `cfg`
         read-write because it is the writer of the configuration, `cfgack`
         read-only because the acknowledgement is the consumer's statement and a
         publisher that could forge one would release a generation nobody had
         staged, which is the entire point of there being two phases — plus the
         four every domain here holds: `clock` read-only to stamp what it says,
         its own `log_config` ring read-write with `log_config_consume`
         read-only, and its own `stats_config` page read-write.

         It holds NO device capability, NO buffer pool and NO dataplane ring, so
         the domain that parses attacker-supplied XML cannot reach a frame, a
         NIC, or the memory either travels through. That is the reason this is a
         domain of its own rather than a function in the forwarder.

         Priority 3, above the forwarder's 2 and the drivers' 1, and the
         ordering is load-bearing in one direction only. The drivers never leave
         `init` — they busy-loop at priority 1 — so a configuration domain at or
         below their priority would be starved of the CPU it needs to publish
         anything at all, and the appliance would come up permanently on
         generation 0. Above them it runs to completion first and then blocks in
         the Microkit event loop, where it costs nothing: it has no polling loop
         and no work except when the consumer acknowledges, so being the highest
         priority in the system cannot starve the dataplane.

         cached="true" on both, as everywhere: they are ordinary memory shared
         between two domains and no device decodes them.

         ITS STACK IS THE LARGEST OF ANY DOMAIN HOLDING NO FRAME BUFFER, and for
         a reason that is not obvious from what it maps: a commit has three whole
         configuration models live at once — the datastore's running and candidate
         pair, and the one staging hands back — plus the byte image built from the
         third, and every one of them carries the 256 rule slots the filter added.
         That took the peak past 0x10000 and faulted this domain on the release
         image with `potential stack overflow, fault address within one page
         outside of stack region`, leaving the appliance permanently on generation
         0. `pd_runtime`'s
         `the_configuration_domains_state_fits_the_stack_it_is_declared_with`
         holds the number below to those four types, so the next entity added to a
         configuration fails a host test rather than a boot. Memory rather than
         authority: nothing this domain may reach changes.

         IT GREW AGAIN WHEN THE DATASTORE STOPPED BEING A LOCAL. Until a document
         could be submitted there was no path to a second commit, so the store was
         dropped at the end of `init` and only a call frame held it; now it is a
         field, and beside it a 64 KiB scratch buffer — one document copied out of
         the peer-written request region, or one rendered into it for a read, never
         both at once. Those two are resident for the domain's life and the three
         models a commit still needs are on top of them, which took the peak past
         the previous 0x40000's twofold headroom. The same host test holds this
         number to the same types, so the next thing added to a configuration
         fails on a developer's machine rather than as a domain that faults on the
         first submission. Memory rather than authority again: what this domain may
         reach is the two rows below and nothing else. -->
    <protection_domain name="config" priority="3" stack_size="0x80000">
        <program_image path="config-pd.elf" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfgack" vaddr="0x3_008_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfgack_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg_request" vaddr="0x3_100_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg_reply" vaddr="0x3_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="chan_cfg_request" vaddr="0x3_300_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="chan_cfg_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="chan_cfg_reply" vaddr="0x3_400_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="chan_cfg_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_config" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The clock domain: it establishes what time it is, says so, and from then
         on announces that time has passed.

         It maps the HPET page, holds an I/O-port capability for the CMOS
         real-time clock, calibrates the timestamp counter against the timer
         whose rate is self-describing, reads the RTC once for an epoch to
         anchor that counter to, publishes one record, and arms one of the
         block's comparators to raise an interrupt on a period. The protection
         domain is documented in pds/clock.

         =================================================================
         THE SYSTEM'S FIRST `<irq>`, AND ITS FIRST PERIODIC WAKEUP
         =================================================================

         Microkit provides no periodic wakeup, and until this row nothing in
         this appliance had one: a protection domain was entered when a frame
         arrived or when a peer signalled it, and a domain holding a deadline
         and no traffic sat at that deadline for ever. That is exactly the shape
         of every schedule the management channel owes — a reconnection backoff,
         an acknowledgement cadence, a flush that owes a send once a second —
         and a silent link is precisely the condition they exist for. So this
         file now builds one out of the hardware it already grants: the domain
         that maps the timer block programs a comparator and takes the interrupt
         it raises.

         WHAT THE `<irq>` GRANTS is one IRQHandler capability, on one I/O APIC
         input. It is a new capability class in this system and it is the
         narrowest instance of it: this domain may acknowledge that one input
         and may do nothing else with it — it cannot mask another, cannot route
         one, and holds no capability over the interrupt controller at all. The
         input is `lfw_hpet::INTERRUPT_PIN`, and this row and that constant are
         one fact stated twice: the domain programs the block to drive the pin
         the grant names, so a pin changed here and not there is a domain armed
         to raise an interrupt nothing delivers, which the build compares.

         WHICH INPUT, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN ARBITRARY CHOICE. The input must be
         one no other device drives. A shared one is an error nothing reports:
         the handler counts another device's interrupts as its own and every
         schedule built on the wakeup runs fast by whatever that device does.
         Input 2 was tried and is wrong — it looks like the legacy controller's
         cascade line and is where a PC-compatible platform delivers the
         interval timer's, which turned a wakeup armed for ten a second into
         thirty. The last input is granted instead, above every legacy line and
         at the top of the block the PCI functions are assigned from. The
         block's own routing capability decides whether it may drive that input
         at all, and a block that says it may not is refused on the console
         rather than programmed on the strength of nothing — and because
         "no other device drives it" is a claim no register answers, the rate is
         measured on the running image rather than argued here.

         EDGE-TRIGGERED, and that is what keeps the wakeup free of device work:
         the block raises the input and lowers it again by itself, so the
         handler owes the kernel an acknowledgement and the device nothing. A
         level-triggered timer would oblige a write to the block's
         interrupt-status register on every tick — a register that domain cannot
         even name.

         WHAT IT COSTS THE DATAPLANE is stated where the priority is, below.

         =================================================================
         `<ioport>` IS NO LONGER A CAPABILITY CLASS ONE DOMAIN HOLDS ALONE
         =================================================================

         READ THE CONSOLE DOMAIN'S `<ioport>` BLOCK BELOW BEFORE THIS ONE. The
         lesson it records was paid for once and applies unchanged here: the
         element places an X86IOPort CAPABILITY in this domain's CNode and does
         NOT unlock an instruction. seL4 leaves the TSS I/O permission bitmap
         denying every port and never edits it, so `in` and `out` executed in
         this domain raise #GP with this grant present and correct; the ports
         below are reached only by invoking the capability through
         `seL4_X86_IOPort_In8` / `seL4_X86_IOPort_Out8`, which is what
         `pds/clock/src/cmos.rs` does and why that file needs no `unsafe`.

         WHAT IT GRANTS: two consecutive I/O ports, 0x70 and 0x71 — the CMOS
         address and data registers, `lfw_rtc::INDEX_PORT` and `DATA_PORT`. That
         crate writes an index to the first and reads the answer from the
         second, and the nine registers it can name are `Register::ALL`, every
         one of them const-asserted to carry bit 7 clear — so no index it can
         form disables the non-maskable interrupt, and no address it can form
         leaves this row.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD is everything the console's block already
         enumerates, minus the CMOS pair this row now grants: no PCI
         configuration address/data pair at 0xCF8/0xCFC, no PS/2 controller, no
         PIC, no PIT, no debug port, and no serial controller — this domain
         cannot write the console it publishes through, which is the whole point
         of publishing through a ring. The drivers and the forwarder still hold
         zero I/O ports between them.

         What the *pair* of grants costs, stated because it is the property that
         changed: the console's claim to be the system's only port holder is
         gone, and with it the argument that an attacker reaching any
         non-console domain reaches no port instruction the kernel will accept.
         What survives is narrower and still worth having — the two windows are
         disjoint, each is held by exactly one domain, and neither domain can
         reach the other's device. In particular this domain cannot splice bytes
         into a console line, and the console cannot read or stop the clock.

         WHY THE CMOS IS A DOMAIN OF ITS OWN rather than a function in the
         console, which already holds a port capability: the two devices are
         unrelated, and merging them would hand the domain that drives the
         operator's only output channel a second device — and hand the domain
         that reads a battery-backed register file the ability to write the line
         an operator reads its result on. The split is what makes "the console
         renders what the clock said" a statement about a ring rather than about
         one domain's own bookkeeping.

         PRIORITY 3, EQUAL TO THE CONFIGURATION DOMAIN, and the reasoning is
         that domain's, one step further. The drivers and the console busy-loop
         forever at priority 1 and the forwarder runs at 2, so a domain that
         must run once and publish cannot sit at or below them: it would depend
         on seL4's round-robin against three domains that never yield, for the
         whole of a calibration window it is trying to measure real time across.
         Above them it runs to completion and then blocks in the Microkit event
         loop — where, now, exactly one thing can reach it.

         Equal to the configuration domain rather than above it. The
         configuration domain leaves `init` for good and this one no longer
         does, so the two can no longer starve each other only because neither
         ever runs long: a tick is a fixed handful of instructions with no loop
         in it, and a configuration commit is a bounded pass. Adding a fourth
         priority level would state an ordering between the two that nothing
         needs — no record either writes depends on the other, and the console
         renders both whenever it gets to them.

         What that priority costs, named rather than left to be measured: a
         calibration window is about a millisecond of real time during which
         this domain preempts the dataplane, once, at boot, before any traffic
         exists — and after it, one preemption per tick period for the life of
         the node, each of them that fixed handful plus the wakeup it hands to
         the management domain. The period is `pds/clock`'s to choose and it
         derives it there from the obligations that rest on it.

         It maps NO pool, NO dataplane ring, NO configuration region, NO ECAM
         page and NO BAR window, so a compromised clock reaches no frame, no NIC
         and no configuration — the shape of isolation the configuration and
         console domains already have, in a third direction. The one region it
         maps that nothing else in the system does is `hpet`. Everything else it
         maps is shared, and by design: `clock` (read-write) is the calibration
         it publishes, and it is the sole writer of a region eight other domains
         map read-only — see the note beside that region — while `log_clock`,
         `log_clock_consume` and `stats_clock` are its own record ring and
         counter page, paired with the console and the management domain exactly
         as every other domain's are. -->
    <protection_domain name="clock" priority="3" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="clock.elf" />
        <ioport id="0" addr="0x70" size="2" />
        <irq id="0" ioapic="0" pin="23" vector="0" trigger="edge" polarity="high" />
        <map mr="hpet" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="hpet_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_clock" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The management domain: the ADDRESSED endpoint of the management port. It
         answers ARP requests for its own address, ICMP echo requests to it, and
         TCP connections to its one listening port; it counts what the port
         receives, and forwards nothing — nothing in this description gives it the
         means to.

         IT NOW READS TWO INSTRUCTIONS AND STILL HOLDS NO CAPABILITY FOR EITHER.
         `RDTSC` once per wakeup, for the instant its transport's timers are
         stated against, and `RDRAND` once at start-up, for the secret those
         connections\' initial sequence numbers are derived from (RFC 6528). Both
         are unprivileged instructions rather than capabilities — no `<ioport>`,
         no `<irq>`, no mapping — so nothing in this file grants or could withhold
         them, and a part with no `RDRAND` refuses this domain rather than
         answering it with a predictable sequence number. That refusal is a
         console record, and pds/management is where the argument lives.

         =================================================================
         WHY THIS DOMAIN IS THE RETURNER ON ONE PIPELINE AND THE OWNER ON
         THE OTHER
         =================================================================

         The forwarder maps NEITHER dataplane `free` ring, and the note beside
         those regions says why: a forged return puts a live DMA target back onto
         an owner's free stack to be handed out a second time while a NIC is
         still writing it. That withholding is affordable there because a
         dataplane descriptor travels ONWARD — the egress driver is what returns
         the buffer, and it is the only domain that can.

         A terminal port has no egress driver, so that argument does not
         transfer, and the choice is not between granting the ring and
         withholding it. It is between granting it and leaking the pool: a
         consumed buffer with no path back is a buffer gone from the driver's
         ledger for good, and 64 frames later the port stops receiving. So
         mgmt_rx_free is granted READ-WRITE here, and what keeps that from being
         the authority the forwarder was refused is WHICH SIDE OF THE RING it is:
         this domain PRODUCES returns and nic_driver2 CONSUMES them as the pool's
         owner (`pd_runtime::PoolOwner::reclaim`), which accepts an index only if
         it is in that driver's own lent set and its ledger agrees. A return it
         forges names a buffer the driver never lent and is refused and counted
         there.

         ON THE TRANSMIT PIPELINE THE ROLES ARE EXACTLY REVERSED. A reply is a
         frame this domain originates, so it is the OWNER of mgmt_tx_pool: it
         allocates a buffer, writes the reply into it, lends it on mgmt_tx_fwd,
         and CONSUMES the returns nic_driver2 produces onto mgmt_tx_free. So each
         pool has one owner, each owner holds the ledger for its own pool, and
         each `free` ring has one producer and one consumer — which is what makes
         a forged return refused rather than believed in both directions. The
         dataplane already has this shape between its two drivers; here one domain
         stands at each end.

         WHAT IT MAPS, and why each grant exists:

           * mgmt_rx_fwd (rw) — consume the descriptors the driver publishes.
           * mgmt_rx_free (rw) — produce the returns, per the argument above.
           * mgmt_rx_pool (READ-ONLY) — copy the received frame out to parse it.
             Read is the whole of the authority parsing needs: a frame this
             appliance was sent is never altered, and the reply is composed
             elsewhere. This is the grant the previous note deferred to "the day
             this port parses ARP or IP", and it arrived with that code.
           * mgmt_tx_pool (rw) — write the reply into the buffer it owns.
           * mgmt_tx_fwd (rw) — lend the reply to the driver.
           * mgmt_tx_free (rw) — consume the driver's returns as the pool's owner.
           * cfg (READ-ONLY) — the addressing comes from the configuration
             document. Read-only because a domain that could
             write it would rewrite the addressing it is about to be judged by,
             and because the publisher must be able to read back what it wrote.
           * clock (READ-ONLY) — what a counter reading means. Every timer of the
             transport on this port is stated against an instant, and an instant
             is a `RDTSC` reading converted by the triple the clock domain
             published. Read-only because a domain that could write it would move
             this node's own idea of time from the one domain that answers the
             management-plane attacker; see the note beside that region.
           * dl_request (rw) — the download this port has been asked for. It is
             this domain's own statement of what an operator wants, so it is the
             only domain that may write it.
           * dl_reply (READ-ONLY) — the window of recorded bytes the recorder
             answered with. Read-only for the reason `cfg` is: the answer is the
             other domain's, and one that could write it would serve an operator
             a recording no medium holds while the recorder reported having
             served the one it does.
           * stats_recorder, stats_hardware_probe and stats_crypto (READ-ONLY)
             — the ninth, tenth and eleventh shards, on exactly the terms of
             the other seven that are not this domain's own.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD, and it is the whole of the management/dataplane
         isolation: NO dataplane region of any kind — not a pool, not a
         `ForwardRings`, not a return ring — NO ECAM page, NO BAR window, NO
         virtqueue and NO ioport. A compromise of the domain that will one day
         terminate an operator's TLS session therefore reaches no frame in flight
         and no NIC.

         AND NO BLOCK REGION EITHER, which the download handover is what makes
         affordable. This domain maps no `blk_io`, no `blk_dma`, no `bar3` and no
         `ecam3`: it never reaches the medium, only the bounded reply the
         recorder composed for the range it asked about. The rejected design is
         the cheaper one — hand it a read grant on the staging window and answer
         a download out of that — and it fails on exactly the property this port
         exists to have: that window holds whatever the recorder is staging at
         the moment, so reading it is reading the capture rather than the
         download. It maps no `tap` either, in either direction, so it cannot
         watch the dataplane's observations go past on their way to the medium.

         AND NO `cfgack`, WHICH IS THE ONE WORTH DWELLING ON. This domain is not
         the consumer of the two-phase commit: it reads the COMMITTED generation
         alone (`pd_runtime::CommittedReader`), so it cannot delay a commit,
         cannot refuse one on anybody's behalf, and holds no region an
         acknowledgement could be forged in. See the note beside cfg/cfgack.

         PRIORITY 2, EQUAL TO THE FORWARDER, and for the forwarder's reason: this
         is a `notified`-driven domain among three drivers that busy-loop forever
         at priority 1, so at or below them a wakeup would wait on seL4's
         round-robin against domains that never yield. Above them it preempts on
         notification, drains, and returns to the event loop. Equal to the
         forwarder rather than above or below it because neither can starve the
         other — each runs to completion per wakeup — and an ordering between the
         two would be a claim nothing needs: no frame crosses from one to the
         other, which is the point of the port.

         STACK 0x80000, and it is the HTTP server that decides it. This domain
         holds, in the value `init` returns and therefore on its stack: two
         `BUFFER_SIZE` scratch buffers (the frame snapshotted out of the pool and
         the reply composed into storage of its own, the pool exposing no safe
         path to its bytes), a request slot of `lfw_http::MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` per
         connection, one `lfw_ip_endpoint::http::RESPONSE_CAPACITY` staging
         buffer — the longest body the management surface answers with, and a
         response composed anywhere else could not be retransmitted — and, since
         recordings became downloadable, one `wire::DOWNLOAD_WINDOW_LEN` window
         of 32 KiB, which is where a recorder's reply is copied before the
         transport takes it. Beside the handler, and not in it, a
         `lfw_metrics::Snapshot` of about 36 KiB: every shard read whole, once
         per reading, in the frame that composes it.

         THE NUMBER HAS DOUBLED TWICE, BOTH TIMES BECAUSE THE MEASURED PEAK
         OVERRAN THE OLD ONE, and both times the same way: the release image
         faulted the domain with `potential stack overflow, fault address within
         one page outside of stack region` and the appliance stopped forwarding.
         The download window took it past 0x40000; the filter took it past
         0x80000, because one shard slot per rule the ABI admits grew both the
         staging buffer and the snapshot, and `init` moves the handler into place
         more than once on the way out of the function. A mebibyte leaves the
         margin an unmeasured peak needs, and
         `pd_runtime`'s `the_management_domains_state_fits_the_stack_it_is_declared_with`
         is what will fail before a boot does next time. It is memory rather than
         authority: no mapping, no grant and no other domain's stack moves. -->
    <protection_domain name="management" priority="2" stack_size="0x100000">
        <program_image path="management.elf" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_free" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_pool" vaddr="0x2_004_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_free" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_pool" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="dl_request" vaddr="0x2_600_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dl_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="dl_reply" vaddr="0x2_700_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dl_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="relay_request" vaddr="0x2_800_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="relay_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="relay_reply" vaddr="0x2_900_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="relay_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg_request" vaddr="0x3_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg_reply" vaddr="0x3_200_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="endpoint" vaddr="0x3_00b_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="endpoint_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_forwarder" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_forwarder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x5_001_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x5_002_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x5_003_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver2_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_management" vaddr="0x5_004_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_management_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_console" vaddr="0x5_005_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_console_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_config" vaddr="0x5_006_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_config_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_clock" vaddr="0x5_007_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_recorder" vaddr="0x5_008_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_recorder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_hardware_probe" vaddr="0x5_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_hardware_probe_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_crypto" vaddr="0x5_00a_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_crypto_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_store" vaddr="0x5_00b_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_store_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_relay" vaddr="0x5_00c_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_relay_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Each driver maps five of its six pipeline regions. The exception is the
         pool it receives INTO: it hands that pool's physical address to its NIC
         as a DMA target and never dereferences a byte of it, so it takes the
         `setvar region_paddr` with no `map` at all. Microkit 2.3.0 patches a
         `region_paddr` whether or not the same PD maps the region (verified
         against the tool, which accepts this description and still emits the
         region's frames); the only thing it refuses is a `region_paddr` for a
         memory region with no `phys_addr`, which every region above fixes.

         The pool a driver transmits OUT of is mapped, because `TxPath::post`
         writes the 12-byte virtio-net header in front of the frame — the only
         production dereference of pool bytes anywhere in the system. -->
    <protection_domain name="nic_driver0" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam0" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar0" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq0" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free0" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free1" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool1" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar0" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq0" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool0" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool1" />
    </protection_domain>

    <protection_domain name="nic_driver1" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam1" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar1" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq1" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free1" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free0" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool0" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar1" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq1" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool1" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool0" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The third instance of the same binary, driving the MANAGEMENT port. It
         differs from the two above in its regions and its peer and in nothing
         else: no `nic-driver.elf` byte distinguishes a management port from a
         dataplane one, which is why this row exists rather than a fourth
         program image (pds/nic-driver's crate header).

         The two pipelines are the management port's own, so no dataplane region
         appears here and this driver can reach no frame belonging to either
         dataplane pipeline. Its receive pool it does not map at all: it takes
         `rx_pool_paddr` and hands it to the NIC, exactly as the two above do with
         theirs — the management domain reads that pool read-only, which is a
         grant to a domain that dereferences a frame and not to the one that DMAs
         into it. Its transmit pool IS mapped, for the header write, and the
         management domain now queues replies onto mgmt_tx_fwd, so this driver's
         transmit side carries traffic rather than existing for the sake of one
         binary. -->
    <protection_domain name="nic_driver2" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam2" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar2" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq2" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_free" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_free" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_pool" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar2" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq2" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="mgmt_rx_pool" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="mgmt_tx_pool" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The recorder: the one domain in this system that owns a BLOCK device,
         and the only one that can put a byte on persistent storage.

         It brings a virtio-blk device up at the pinned function 00:05.0, proves
         the path to the medium by reading a sector and writing a recognisable
         one back (`lfw_blk::smoke`), and then idles. What it will do next —
         encode the tap's observations as pcapng onto a segmented ring, and serve
         a byte range of that ring back through the download handover — is the
         next milestone; the regions it needs for both are granted here already,
         and pds/recorder records which of them its code does not yet read.

         =================================================================
         WHAT IT MAPS, AND WHY EACH GRANT IS THE NARROWEST ONE THAT WORKS
         =================================================================

           * ecam3 (rw, uncached) — the 4 KiB configuration page of its one
             pinned PCI function. It performs no enumeration and holds no other
             function's page, so a device at any other address on the bus is
             unreachable from here, exactly as it is from each NIC driver.
           * bar3 (rw, uncached) — the window its device's BAR is relocated to.
           * blk_dma (rw, cached) — the virtqueue, the per-request headers and
             the status bytes the device writes. Driver bookkeeping; no payload.
           * blk_io (rw, cached) — the staging window every recorded byte passes
             through. See the note beside that region on why the management
             domain does not map it.
           * tap (READ-ONLY) / tap_consume (rw) — the capture ring, mirrored
             against the forwarder's grant. This domain reads observations and
             publishes how far it has read; it cannot write one, which is what
             makes a stored capture the forwarder's testimony rather than this
             domain's.
           * dl_request (READ-ONLY) / dl_reply (rw) — the download handover,
             mirrored against the management domain's grant. It reads what was
             asked for and writes the answer.
           * clock (READ-ONLY) — what a counter reading means, for the instant
             every log record and every recorded observation is dated with.
           * log_recorder (rw) / log_recorder_consume (READ-ONLY) — its own
             console ring, on the same two-region footing as every other writer.
           * stats_recorder (rw) — its own shard, which the management domain
             reads and cannot write.

         =================================================================
         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD, AND IT IS MOST OF THE SYSTEM
         =================================================================

         NO DATAPLANE POOL, of either direction and of either kind — not pool0,
         not pool1, not mgmt_rx_pool, not mgmt_tx_pool. This is the grant that
         matters most, because a recording appliance is exactly the component
         that has a plausible-sounding reason to want one: "to capture a frame
         you must read the frame". It is refused, and the tap is the answer —
         what crosses to this domain is an annotation the forwarder composed, so
         a compromised recorder cannot read a frame in flight, cannot corrupt one
         under the routing decision that inspected it, and cannot reach the
         memory a NIC is DMAing into.

         NO `ForwardRings` AND NO `ReturnRing`, of any pipeline. It is not on any
         frame's path, so it can neither delay one nor forge a buffer return —
         the move the forwarder itself is refused, for the same reason.

         NO NIC REGION AT ALL: not ecam0..ecam2, not bar0..bar2, not vq0..vq2. A
         compromised recorder reaches no network device, so an attacker who
         reaches the domain that owns the disk does not thereby reach the wire.
         The converse holds by the same rows: no driver maps a block region, so a
         compromised NIC driver cannot write the medium.

         NO `<ioport>`. The two windows this system grants belong to the console
         and the clock; this domain holds neither, so `in` and `out` are as
         unavailable to it as they are to the forwarder and the drivers. A block
         device reached by port invocation is a device this domain cannot reach.

         NO `cfgack`, on the management domain's terms exactly: it reads the
         COMMITTED generation to learn the interface names a recording must
         carry, takes no part in the two-phase commit, cannot delay one, and
         holds no region an acknowledgement could be forged in.

         NO OTHER DOMAIN'S LOG RING and NO OTHER DOMAIN'S SHARD, in either
         direction, which is what every writer here is held to.

         PRIORITY 1, EQUAL TO THE DRIVERS AND THE CONSOLE, and for their reason.
         This domain never leaves `init`: Microkit has no periodic wakeup, it
         takes no interrupt from its device by design (no MSI-X, no INTx), and it
         must poll both its request queue and the tap ring, so it busy-loops. At
         a higher priority it would preempt the dataplane for as long as it held
         the CPU — and a block completion can be milliseconds away, which is a
         very long time to hold a forwarder off. Equal to them, seL4 round-robins
         the four at priority 1 and this domain's share is a slice rather than a
         stall; the forwarder at 2 and the configuration and clock domains at 3
         still preempt it.

         What that costs, named rather than left to be measured: a device that
         withholds a completion cannot be waited on indefinitely here, because
         waiting is spending a shared timeslice. `lfw_blk::smoke::POLL_BUDGET` is
         what bounds it — a driver constant, not a device-controlled
         condition — and a device that exhausts it parks this domain on a refusal
         rather than starving the other three.

         STACK 0x8000, the drivers'. This domain holds a sector-sized buffer or
         two on its stack at a time and nothing like the management domain's
         response staging: the recording itself lives in `blk_io`, which is a
         mapped region and not stack.

         `cfg` ALONE CARRIES NO `setvar_vaddr`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE

         Every other region this domain maps is patched into a symbol the
         program image declares. `cfg` is not: the recording's interface names
         come from the configuration document, and the code that reads them is
         a later change. The Microkit tool refuses a `setvar_vaddr` naming a
         symbol no program image defines, so the attribute arrives with the code
         that reads it and never before it — a symbol declared and left unread
         would be machinery with no consumer. The address is fixed here
         and the region is granted and mapped at it either way; README's status
         records what is still deferred.

         THE THREE `setvar region_paddr`s are the physical bases its device is
         programmed with: the BAR it is relocated to, the DMA region the
         virtqueue is placed in, and the staging window every data segment names.
         Unlike a NIC driver, this domain MAPS all three — there is no equivalent
         of the receive pool it hands over and never dereferences, because it is
         both the composer and the reader of everything its device transfers. -->
    <protection_domain name="recorder" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="recorder.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam3" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar3" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="blk_dma" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dma_vaddr" />
        <map mr="blk_io" vaddr="0x10_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="io_vaddr" />
        <map mr="tap" vaddr="0x2_400_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tap_vaddr" />
        <map mr="tap_consume" vaddr="0x2_500_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tap_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="dl_request" vaddr="0x2_600_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dl_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="dl_reply" vaddr="0x2_700_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dl_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_recorder" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_recorder_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_relay" vaddr="0x4_b00_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_relay_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_relay_consume" vaddr="0x4_b10_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_relay_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_recorder" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_relay" vaddr="0x5_00c_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_relay_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar3" />
        <setvar symbol="dma_paddr" region_paddr="blk_dma" />
        <setvar symbol="io_paddr" region_paddr="blk_io" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The hardware probe: the FIRST domain compiled with the hardfloat SIMD
         target, and the one the other two rest on. It proves at boot that such a
         domain loads and runs under this kernel at all, that AES-NI and PCLMULQDQ answer their known answers,
         and that XMM state survives the kernel's context switches; it reports
         one record and parks. The protection domain is documented in
         pds/hardware-probe, and the cryptography domain is designed on what
         this domain's record establishes.

         WHAT IT MAPS is the minimum a reporting domain can hold: its own log
         ring read-write with its consume cursor read-only, its own statistics
         shard read-write, and the clock calibration read-only to stamp its
         records — the same four grants every domain here carries and nothing
         else. NO device region of any kind — no ECAM page, no BAR window, no
         virtqueue, no block region — NO pool, NO dataplane or management ring,
         NO configuration region, NO tap, NO download region and NO `<ioport>`.
         The instructions under test are unprivileged and carried by no
         capability, exactly as the management domain's `RDRAND` and every
         domain's `RDTSC` are, so nothing in this file could grant or withhold
         them; what this row grants is only the means to say what happened.

         NO CHANNEL IN EITHER DIRECTION: it runs to completion in `init` and
         parks where nothing can reach it, exactly as the clock domain does.

         PRIORITY 1, EQUAL TO THE BUSY-LOOP DOMAINS, and here the equality is
         the experiment rather than a scheduling compromise: seL4 round-robins
         the priority-1 domains, so the probe's check loop is preempted
         mid-pass by domains that never yield — which is precisely the context
         switch whose XMM save/restore is under test. A higher priority would
         let the loop run unpreempted to its budget and prove nothing about
         state the kernel never had to save. The loop's work is bounded by its
         own compile-time budgets and the domain then blocks for good, so it
         cannot starve the domains it shares the priority with beyond a
         fraction of a second of boot. -->
    <protection_domain name="hardware_probe" priority="1" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="hardware-probe.elf" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_hardware_probe" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_hardware_probe_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_hardware_probe" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The cryptography domain: the second binary compiled with the hardfloat
         SIMD target, and the reason that target exists. It proves at boot that
         every cryptographic primitive this appliance owns answers its
         published NIST CAVP, RFC and Wycheproof vectors on this part, measures
         what each costs in cycles per byte, seeds a deterministic random bit
         generator from RDRAND, reports all of it, and parks. The protection
         domain is documented in pds/crypto.

         WHAT IT MAPS is the reporting minimum the hardware probe holds, its
         allocator, and the delegation: its own log ring read-write with its
         consume cursor read-only, its own statistics shard read-write, the clock
         calibration read-only to stamp its records, `arena_crypto` read-write,
         `sign_request` read-write, `sign_reply` READ-ONLY and `endpoint`
         READ-ONLY. NO device region of
         any kind — no ECAM page, no BAR window, no virtqueue, no block region —
         NO pool, NO dataplane or management ring, NO configuration region, NO
         tap, NO download region and NO `<ioport>`. `RDRAND`, `CPUID` and `RDTSC`
         are unprivileged and carried by no capability, exactly as the management
         domain's `RDRAND` and every domain's `RDTSC` are, so nothing in this file
         could grant or withhold them; what this row grants is only the means to
         say what happened, to allocate inside its own bound, and to ask.

         AND IT READS WHERE THE APPLIANCE DIALS, which is the row added with the
         management channel's client. `endpoint` is READ-ONLY here, exactly as it
         is in the domain that dials, and the reason it is here at all is that
         this domain is the one that VALIDATES: a management server's certificate
         is held to the address literal that was dialled and to no name, so the
         address is half of a trust decision and must not arrive from the domain
         that owns the network. The relay's ABI has no field for one either, and
         that stays true. What the mapping grants is the ability to read an
         address and a port that authenticate nothing.

         WHY IT HOLDS NO STORE DEVICE, though it authenticates under the key that
         medium carries: the device private key must never leave the domain that
         owns the medium, because a key in two domains is a key whose custody
         nobody can state. So this domain holds NONE of `ecam4`, `bar4`,
         `blk2_dma` or `blk2_io`, and reaches the key only as a capability to USE
         it — the two `sign_*` rows above, whose ABI has no field a scalar fits in
         and whose grants make the holder the only writer of the answer. What this
         domain can do with the delegation is ask for a signature; what it cannot
         do is learn what produced one.

         AND IT MAY WAKE THE HOLDER, which is the one send capability this row
         grants: `sign` is called synchronously from inside a handshake and has no
         continuation a reply could be delivered to, so the holder is signalled
         and the answer is read. The reverse direction is refused below.

         PRIORITY 2, ABOVE THE BUSY-LOOP DOMAINS AND BELOW BOTH THE CLOCK AND THE
         KEY HOLDER, and every part of that is deliberate. Above priority 1,
         because the throughput figure this domain reports is a measurement and a
         domain that never yields would otherwise be measured along with the
         cipher — the reported figure is the least any round spent, so a
         preemption can only inflate it, but not competing for the slice in the
         first place is what makes the number worth reporting. Below the clock at
         priority 3, because this domain stamps its records against a calibration
         that domain publishes, and a stamp is worth more than the few
         milliseconds of ordering it costs. BELOW THE STORE DOMAIN, also at 3, for
         a reason that is a correctness premise rather than a preference: this
         domain reads for a reply in a bounded spin, and a holder at or below its
         own priority would never run to write one. See the store domain's row.
         Its work is bounded by its own compile-time constants — a fixed vector
         corpus, a fixed number of timing rounds, and the delegation's own read
         budget — and the domain then blocks for good.

         THE STACK IS 384 KiB, AND IT IS A BUDGET RATHER THAN A NUMBER. At 128 KiB
         no package could be installed on this image at all: the first upload to
         reach the document check faulted, and it faulted the same way every time
         because nothing about it depends on what the peer sent. The arithmetic,
         measured off the faulting boot rather than estimated:

           60,856 bytes  spent before the document reader is entered — the relay's
                         turn, the TLS record layer under it, the onboarding
                         surface, and the upload that reads the staged archive back
           82,056 bytes  the document reader's own frame: 48 bytes of saved
                         registers, an 80 KiB probe span and 88 bytes of residual
          ══════════════
          142,912 bytes  demanded, against 131,072 declared — an overshoot of
                         11,840, which the probe turns into a clean data fault one
                         page under the stack rather than into a silent write

         So 393,216 leaves 250,304 bytes of headroom, a factor of 2.75 on the
         deepest path measured — and every other path this domain runs, the vector
         corpus and the handshake included, fitted the 128 KiB it had for as long
         as it had it. That factor is the point and the number is its consequence:
         the first term is third-party depth that moves when the TLS stack is
         bumped, and the second is a constant of the document reader that moves
         when the configuration model grows. Half of the budget is therefore held
         by `lfw_package`'s
         `reading_a_package_fits_the_stack_the_cryptography_domain_is_declared_with`,
         which sizes the model and the package it hands back and fails on a
         developer's machine long before either could reach a booted node.

         WHY IT WAS URGENT rather than a tuning matter: the faulting path is
         reachable by the unauthenticated peer this port faces. A device
         certificate need only carry this appliance's public key, which the
         appliance publishes on its own page and in its request, so an attacker
         signs one with an authority of their own and the archive reaches the
         document check on its way to being refused. That made a remote fault of
         the domain holding the device key and the appliance's only allocator.

         MEMORY RATHER THAN AUTHORITY. The stack budget above changed nothing
         this domain may reach: no region added or removed, no mapping's extent,
         cacheability or permissions altered, no channel and no send capability
         touched. What it added to the generated report was 64
         `frame_crypto_stack` pages and nothing else. -->
    <protection_domain name="crypto" priority="2" stack_size="0x60000">
        <program_image path="crypto.elf" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_crypto" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_crypto_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_crypto" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <map mr="arena_crypto" vaddr="0x6_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="arena_vaddr" />
        <map mr="sign_request" vaddr="0x7_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="sign_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="sign_reply" vaddr="0x7_100_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="sign_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="relay_request" vaddr="0x7_200_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="relay_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="relay_reply" vaddr="0x7_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="relay_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="install_staging" vaddr="0x7_400_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="install_staging_vaddr" />
        <map mr="endpoint" vaddr="0x3_00b_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="endpoint_vaddr" />
        <map mr="chan_cfg_request" vaddr="0x3_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="chan_cfg_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="chan_cfg_reply" vaddr="0x3_400_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="chan_cfg_reply_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The store domain: the one holder of the appliance's own identity, and
         the third binary compiled with the hardfloat SIMD target.

         WHAT IT IS FOR. It owns a SECOND virtio-blk device — function 00:06.0,
         separate from the recorder's 00:05.0 — and on it the appliance's
         persistent state: the device identifier, the P-256 keypair, the
         self-signed onboarding certificate binding the two, the delivered trust
         anchor and endpoint an owner leaves, and the configuration slot array.
         On a fresh medium it mints that identity and writes it durably; on every
         later boot it loads it, holds it to itself, reports which appliance
         returned, and parks. The protection domain is documented in pds/store.

         AND IT INSTALLS AN OWNER, which is the second thing that writes this
         medium and the reason it now holds the device for its whole life. An
         administrator's onboarding package crosses in `install_staging`, which
         this domain maps READ-ONLY, and a fourth delegation operation asks it to
         take ownership: it snapshots that region into its own `blk2_io` window,
         holds the package to every rule of the package contract, compares the
         device certificate against the point in its OWN record rather than
         against anything a peer offered, verifies one signature under one
         profile, and writes the result into the copy the generation's parity
         selects behind a flush. Nothing routes an upload to it yet.

         AND IT SIGNS UNDER THAT IDENTITY, which is what it gained and the whole
         reason its priority moved. The domain that terminates a
         mutually-authenticated session needs a signature under this key on every
         handshake and must never hold the key, so this domain keeps the keypair
         after establishing it and answers a signing delegation over
         `sign_request`/`sign_reply`. It no longer parks beyond reach: it parks in
         the Microkit event loop and the asking domain wakes it.

         WHAT IT MAPS is the second block device, the four grants every reporting
         domain here carries, the delegation's two regions and the staging region
         it reads a package out of, and nothing else.
         Its ECAM page, its BAR window, its DMA region and its staging window are
         `ecam4`, `bar4`, `blk2_dma` and `blk2_io`, and NO other domain maps any of
         them — not the recorder, which owns the other block device and no part of
         this one. It maps NO ecam0..3, NO bar0..3, NO blk_dma and NO blk_io, so
         the domain that holds the private scalar cannot read or write a recording
         and the domain that answers a download cannot read the scalar. It maps NO
         network region of any kind — no vq, no buffer pool of either dataplane or
         management pipeline, no `ForwardRings`, no `ReturnRing` — NO configuration
         region, NO tap, NO download region, and NO `<ioport>`.

         WHY THAT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE ELSE. The private scalar is
         plaintext on this medium, deliberately and for want of anywhere to keep
         a wrapping key, so physical possession of the store IS identity theft —
         and this row is what makes possession the ONLY way to it. There is still
         no path from a packet to these bytes: no domain that terminates a
         connection maps any part of this device, and this domain's ONE channel
         goes to the cryptography domain, which holds no network region either. A
         compromise would have to traverse a domain no frame reaches, to arrive at
         an ABI with no field for a key.

         ONE CHANNEL, RECEIVE ONLY, and both halves are stated where they are
         granted below. It is woken by the domain that asks, and it holds NO send
         capability on anything: it answers by publishing into a region that domain
         reads, so there is nothing a reverse signal would tell it. It still draws
         its own randomness — `RDRAND` and `CPUID` are unprivileged and carried by
         no capability, so this file can neither grant nor withhold them — which is
         the point: a seed taken over a channel would let the domain at the other
         end reproduce the device key. That is the difference between the channel
         it now has and the one it must never have: a request to USE the key, and
         never a path to the material behind it.

         PRIORITY 3, WITH THE RUN-TO-COMPLETION-THEN-PARK DOMAINS, AND IT WAS 1.
         The old argument was the recorder's — it polls its device for a completion
         rather than blocking on one, and a higher priority would let it spin
         against domains that never yield — and it was written when this domain
         held no channel and served nobody. WHAT CHANGED is that it now answers a
         request from a domain at priority 2. A responder BELOW its requester
         cannot answer it: the cryptography domain reads for the reply in a bounded
         spin, because `sign` is called synchronously inside a handshake and has no
         continuation a notification could resume, and a holder at priority 1 would
         not be scheduled until that spin gave up. The delegation would refuse on
         every boot, deterministically. So the holder sits ABOVE the asker: a
         notification then preempts into this domain immediately, it computes and
         publishes, and the asker's next read finds the answer — the spin
         terminating on its first iteration in practice, with its budget there for
         the byzantine case.

         WHAT THE HIGHER PRIORITY COSTS, stated rather than waved past: while this
         domain polls its device it now preempts the dataplane. That happens at
         boot, during onboarding, and on a configuration commit, each a bounded
         transfer of a few kilobytes; the dataplane keeps forwarding the last
         committed configuration either side of it, because a table is switched
         between polls and is never half-applied. It is NOT a protected call and
         this description grows no message-passing IPC: notification plus shared
         memory under a strict protocol is the pattern here, and a synchronous call
         would hand the caller's scheduling context to the domain that owns the
         appliance's identity.

         WHAT STILL BOUNDS IT is unchanged and is what makes the cost payable:
         `lfw_blk`'s own poll budget and nothing the device controls, so a hostile
         or dead device cannot hold the higher priority for longer than that budget
         however it misbehaves. Two of the three things it serves wait on nothing
         at all — a signature and a certificate are a demand taken, answered and
         published in one pass — and an install waits, because it reads the record
         and writes it back, on transfers that same budget bounds. The number of
         demands one wakeup answers, and the number of installs a boot serves, are
         constants of `pds/store` rather than of how fast a peer writes.

         THE STACK IS 256 KiB, AND IT GREW EIGHTFOLD IN ONE CHANGE. It was 32 KiB
         while this domain established an identity and then held only a keypair:
         the device went away with the establishing run, and the one large frame
         was the 8 KiB the state record crosses in.

         Two things moved it. This domain now holds the device for its whole life,
         because installing an onboarding package writes the medium and that
         happens when a peer asks rather than while the domain is starting. And an
         install READS A CONFIGURATION DOCUMENT: the package carries one, it is
         held to the same reader every document goes through, and that reader's
         model is 24 KiB built and discarded inside a frame that already holds the
         state record twice over and both certificates' fixed storage. At 32 KiB
         and again at 64 KiB the domain took a write fault one page past its stack
         on the boot after the change, which is exactly what a stack sized for the
         old shape produces.

         The number is measured rather than chosen, and it is held to the types:
         `lfw_store`'s `an_install_fits_the_stack_the_store_domain_is_declared_with`
         bounds the composition at half of it, so the next thing added to an
         install fails a host test rather than a boot. Memory rather than
         authority: nothing this domain may reach changes. -->
    <protection_domain name="store" priority="3" stack_size="0x40000">
        <program_image path="store.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam4" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar4" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="blk2_dma" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="dma_vaddr" />
        <map mr="blk2_io" vaddr="0x10_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="io_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_store" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_store_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_store" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <map mr="sign_request" vaddr="0x7_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="sign_request_vaddr" />
        <map mr="sign_reply" vaddr="0x7_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="sign_reply_vaddr" />
        <map mr="install_staging" vaddr="0x7_400_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="install_staging_vaddr" />
        <map mr="owner" vaddr="0x3_00a_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="owner_vaddr" />
        <map mr="endpoint" vaddr="0x3_00b_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="endpoint_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar4" />
        <setvar symbol="dma_paddr" region_paddr="blk2_dma" />
        <setvar symbol="io_paddr" region_paddr="blk2_io" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The console domain: the one holder of the serial controller.

         =================================================================
         `<ioport>` ENTERED THIS SYSTEM HERE
         =================================================================

         Memory mappings and notification channels were the whole of what this
         description granted until this row. It is neither: it places an
         X86IOPort CAPABILITY in the console domain's CNode, and a capability is
         a thing the domain INVOKES. It does not unlock an instruction. seL4 leaves the TSS I/O
         permission bitmap denying every port and never edits it, so `in` and
         `out` executed inside any protection domain raise #GP however this
         file is written; the ports below are reached only by invoking this
         capability through `seL4_X86_IOPort_In8` / `seL4_X86_IOPort_Out8`,
         which is what `pds/console/src/com1.rs` does and why that file needs
         no `unsafe`.

         READ THAT BEFORE ADDING THE NEXT `<ioport>`. The console driver was
         first built on the opposite reading — that this element hands a domain
         a window of the I/O permission bitmap — so it held the capability and
         executed `out %al,(%dx)` against 0x3F9, the first write of the UART
         init sequence. It faulted with #GP, on a grant that was present and
         correct. The grant makes the invocation legal, never the instruction.

         The three attributes are exactly what the Microkit 2.3.0 tool accepts
         — `id`, `addr`, `size`, and nothing else — and the tool refuses the
         element outright off x86 — this project is x86_64 only, with no
         accommodation made for another architecture.

         WHAT IT GRANTS: eight consecutive I/O ports, 0x3F8 through 0x3FF, the
         PC-compatible COM1 window. That is the whole of the first 16550
         controller: its data register, interrupt enable, FIFO control /
         interrupt identification, line control, modem control, line status,
         modem status and scratch. `crates/uart-16550` uses five of the eight
         and forms every address as `COM1_BASE | offset` with the base's
         alignment to an eight-port window asserted at build time, so no address
         it can form leaves this row.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD, and it is nearly everything an I/O-port grant
         could be: no other port in the 65536-port space, so no PCI
         configuration address/data pair at 0xCF8/0xCFC (which would be a second
         path to every device's configuration space, next to the ECAM mappings
         the drivers hold), no PS/2 controller, no PIC, no PIT, no CMOS/RTC, no
         debug port. The CMOS pair belongs to the clock domain above and to it
         alone; this domain cannot read or stop the clock, and that domain
         cannot write the line an operator reads its result on. The drivers and
         the forwarder hold zero ports between them, so an attacker who reaches
         either reaches no port instruction that will execute.

         WHY ONE DOMAIN OWNS IT. The device is unsynchronised and has one
         register file, so several writers interleave their bytes into one
         another and a console line arrives spliced with a second domain's. A
         capability held by exactly one domain is what makes that
         unrepresentable rather than unlikely — and it is a claim about THIS
         window rather than about the port space, which the clock domain's grant
         above shares nothing with. In the RELEASE kernel
         configuration this domain is then the only writer of 0x3F8 in the whole
         system and the property is exact. In the DEBUG configuration it is not:
         that kernel is built with CONFIG_PRINTING and writes the same port for
         its own boot banner and fault reports. That is accepted — the kernel
         prints on boot and on faults, not per record — but it is the reason the
         single-writer claim is stated of the shipped profile.

         WHAT IT MAPS: twenty-two log regions in eleven pairs, and the perms are the
         whole of the argument, as they are for the forwarder's `cfg`/`cfgack`.
         Each `log_<domain>` is READ-ONLY here: this domain renders those
         records and must read them, and a console that could also write one
         could attribute a line to a domain that never emitted it. Each
         `log_<domain>_consume` is read-write because how far the console has
         read is this domain's own statement about itself, and it is the only
         domain that may make it. See the note beside those regions.

         It holds no pool, no dataplane ring, no configuration region, no ECAM
         page and no BAR window — neither block device's included — so a
         compromised console reaches no frame, no
         NIC, and no configuration — the same shape of isolation the
         configuration domain has, in the other direction.

         PRIORITY 1, EQUAL TO THE DRIVERS, and that is a decision rather than a
         leftover. This domain never leaves `init` for the same reason the
         drivers do not: a `notified`-driven console would have to return to the
         event loop after each activation, so a boot transcript longer than the
         UART's 16-byte FIFO would stall until something happened to wake it
         again — and the boot transcript is the whole purpose of a console. The
         clock domain's tick does not answer that: it arrives on a period chosen
         for the management channel's schedules, and a console carrying sixteen
         bytes per tick would take minutes over a boot transcript. It therefore
         busy-polls,
         which makes its priority a scheduling question rather than a
         formality. Above the drivers it would preempt the dataplane for as long
         as it held the CPU, and at 115200 baud a byte occupies the line for
         about 87 microseconds, so a burst of a few lines is milliseconds of
         stalled forwarding. Equal to them, seL4 round-robins the three at
         priority 1 and the console's share is a slice rather than a stall. The
         forwarder at 2 and the configuration domain at 3 still preempt it, so
         nothing on a notification path is delayed by it at all.

         The improvement, recorded rather than built: an interrupt-driven
         transmitter, which would remove the polling entirely. What it needs is a
         second `<irq>` — the serial controller's own transmitter-empty
         interrupt, on an input this file does not grant — and a console that
         returns to the event loop between bursts, which is a different domain
         from this one rather than a flag on it. -->
    <protection_domain name="console" priority="1" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="console.elf" />
        <ioport id="0" addr="0x3f8" size="8" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_009_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_forwarder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_forwarder_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x4_100_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0_consume" vaddr="0x4_110_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver0_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x4_200_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1_consume" vaddr="0x4_210_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver1_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config" vaddr="0x4_300_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_config_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config_consume" vaddr="0x4_310_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_config_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock" vaddr="0x4_400_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock_consume" vaddr="0x4_410_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_clock_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x4_500_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver2_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2_consume" vaddr="0x4_510_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver2_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management" vaddr="0x4_600_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_management_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management_consume" vaddr="0x4_610_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_management_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_recorder" vaddr="0x4_700_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_recorder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_recorder_consume" vaddr="0x4_710_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_recorder_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_hardware_probe" vaddr="0x4_800_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_hardware_probe_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_hardware_probe_consume" vaddr="0x4_810_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_hardware_probe_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_crypto" vaddr="0x4_900_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_crypto_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_crypto_consume" vaddr="0x4_910_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_crypto_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_store" vaddr="0x4_a00_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_store_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_store_consume" vaddr="0x4_a10_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_store_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_relay" vaddr="0x4_b00_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_relay_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_relay_consume" vaddr="0x4_b10_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_relay_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_console" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Both dataplane channels carry notifications in one direction only, so
         only that direction is granted: a driver signals the forwarder that
         frames are queued (pds/nic-driver, `PEER.notify()`), and the forwarder
         answers nothing back — it drains both pipelines on any wakeup and
         returns, and the drivers could not receive a notification anyway, since
         they never leave `init` and so never reach the Microkit event loop.
         `notify` defaults to true on an `<end>`, which would leave the
         forwarder holding a send capability on each driver's notification that
         no code path uses. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver0" id="0" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="0" notify="false" />
    </channel>
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver1" id="0" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="1" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The management port's channel, granted in one direction for exactly the
         reason the two driver channels above are: nic_driver2 signals that
         frames are queued, and the management domain answers nothing back — it
         drains the pipeline on any wakeup and returns, and the driver could not
         receive a notification anyway, never leaving `init`. `notify` defaults to
         true on an `<end>`, which would leave the management domain holding a
         send capability on a driver that has no event loop to observe it. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver2" id="0" />
        <end pd="management" id="0" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The one channel in this system granted in BOTH directions, and it is a
         decision rather than a `notify` nobody thought about — which is why
         both ends state it rather than leaning on Microkit's default of true.

         Applying a configuration is a two-phase commit, and each phase is a
         signal the other end cannot infer. The config domain offers a
         generation and signals; the forwarder re-checks every field of it,
         stages a table, writes its acknowledgement and signals BACK, because
         the config domain has no other way to learn that the generation is safe
         to release — it cannot poll, having no loop to poll from, and it must
         not release a generation the consumer has refused. The config domain
         then publishes the commit and signals again, because the forwarder
         switches tables between two polls and would otherwise not run one until
         the next frame arrived — a node whose configuration lands only once
         traffic starts is a node that is misconfigured for exactly as long as
         it is idle.

         The two driver channels remain one-directional for the reason stated
         above them, and nothing here changes that: this pair grants the
         forwarder a send capability on the config domain alone. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="config" id="0" notify="true" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="2" notify="true" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The download channel, granted in ONE direction only, and the direction
         is the opposite of every other one-way channel here.

         The RECORDER may signal the MANAGEMENT domain: a download window is
         ready in `dl_reply`, and that domain is `notified`-driven — it is woken,
         it drains, it returns — so without the signal it would learn of the
         answer only when the next frame happened to wake it, which for an
         operator waiting on a download is a stall of unbounded length.

         THE MANAGEMENT DOMAIN MAY NOT SIGNAL BACK, and that end states
         notify="false" rather than leaning on Microkit's default of true. The
         recorder busy-polls `dl_request` on every pass of its loop, exactly as
         it polls the tap and its own request queue, so a notification would tell
         it nothing it is not about to read anyway — and a send capability that
         no path consumes is reach granted for nothing. It would also be
         a wakeup capability held by the domain an attacker reaches first on the
         domain that owns the disk, which is authority worth refusing on its own
         terms even if the recorder ever did block.

         This is the management domain's SECOND end, id 1: id 0 is nic_driver2's
         channel, where it is likewise the receiver and likewise holds no send
         capability. So that domain still holds none at all, in this system or on
         any channel, which is what MANAGEMENT_CHANNEL_ONE_WAY claims. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="recorder" id="0" notify="true" />
        <end pd="management" id="1" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The configuration submission channel, and the SECOND channel in this
         system granted in BOTH directions. It is also the channel that ended
         MANAGEMENT_CHANNEL_ONE_WAY: before it the management domain held no send
         capability at all, on any channel, and this was the first. It is now one
         of exactly two — this one and the TLS relay's below, and nothing else.

         WHY MANAGEMENT MUST BE ABLE TO SIGNAL. The config domain has no polling
         loop: it runs `init` to completion and then blocks in the Microkit event
         loop, which is the whole reason it costs nothing at the highest priority
         in the system. A submitted document in `cfg_request` is therefore
         invisible to it until something wakes it, and the only party that knows a
         document has arrived is the domain that copied it in. Making the config
         domain poll instead would burn a core forever to catch an operator action
         that happens once a month.

         WHAT THAT SEND CAPABILITY IS WORTH TO AN ATTACKER, stated plainly because
         it is the one authority this landing hands to the domain an attacker
         reaches first: a wakeup, at whatever rate they choose. What the config
         domain does on a wakeup is bounded — it takes at most one demand per
         change of the requester's sequence and answers it — and the same party
         could provoke exactly that by `POST`ing a document, which is a request
         this appliance is now built to accept. So the capability adds no reach
         beyond what the feature itself grants, and it grants nothing on the
         dataplane: this channel's other end is a domain holding no device, no pool
         and no ring.

         AND WHY CONFIG MUST BE ABLE TO SIGNAL BACK. The management domain is
         `notified`-driven too — it is woken, it drains, it returns — so without
         the reverse direction it would learn that its submission had been decided
         only when the next frame happened to wake it, which for a client holding a
         connection open is a stall of unbounded length. The recorder's channel is
         granted for exactly that reason in exactly that direction.

         Both ends state `notify` rather than leaning on Microkit's default of
         true, as the config/forwarder pair does: a direction that matters is a
         direction written down. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="config" id="1" notify="true" />
        <end pd="management" id="2" notify="true" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The signing delegation's channel, granted in ONE direction: the
         CRYPTOGRAPHY domain may wake the STORE domain, and the store domain may
         signal nothing at all.

         WHY THE ASKER MUST BE ABLE TO SIGNAL. The store domain has no polling
         loop. It establishes an identity in `init` and then blocks in the Microkit
         event loop, which is why it costs nothing at a priority above the
         dataplane's, and a request written into `sign_request` is invisible to it
         until something wakes it. The only party that knows a request exists is
         the domain that wrote it. Making the store domain poll instead would burn
         the highest-but-one priority in the system forever to catch a handshake.

         WHY THE HOLDER MAY NOT SIGNAL BACK, and that end states notify="false"
         rather than leaning on Microkit's default of true. The asking domain calls
         `sign` synchronously from inside a handshake: there is no continuation for
         a notification to resume and no loop for it to be observed in, so the
         reply is read rather than waited for — a bounded read, whose first
         iteration succeeds precisely because the holder sits at a higher priority
         and has already run. A send capability would therefore be authority no
         path consumes. It would also be a wakeup capability held by the domain
         that owns the appliance's identity on the domain that runs adopted
         protocol code, which is worth refusing on its own terms.

         WHAT THAT SEND CAPABILITY IS WORTH TO AN ATTACKER WHO REACHES THE ASKER,
         stated plainly because it is the one authority this landing hands over: a
         wakeup, at whatever rate they choose, on a domain whose answer to one is
         bounded by a constant of its own — at most four demands, each of which
         `wire::SignResponder::take` will only yield once per change of the
         requester's sequence. What they get for it is a signature over bytes they
         chose, which is what a signing oracle is; what they do not get is the key,
         because the reply region's ABI has no field for one and they cannot write
         that region in any case. The domain they can reach is also the one that
         holds no network region, so this capability is not reachable from a
         packet.

         THIS IS THE STORE DOMAIN'S FIRST CHANNEL AND ITS ONLY ONE, in either
         direction, which is what STORE_CHANNEL_RECEIVE_ONLY claims: id 0 is this,
         it is the receiver, and it holds no send capability anywhere in this
         system. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="crypto" id="0" notify="true" />
        <end pd="store" id="0" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The TLS relay's channel, the THIRD granted in BOTH directions and the
         only one of the three whose two ends sit at the SAME PRIORITY. The other
         two each join a priority-3 domain to a priority-2 one, where the higher
         one runs on being woken and the lower one is the side that waits; here
         neither side is the scheduler's favourite, which is what the next
         paragraph is about.

         WHY BOTH ENDS MAY SIGNAL. The two domains sit at the SAME PRIORITY and
         both are event-driven: neither is scheduled while the other holds the
         processor, and neither has a polling loop a peer's write could be
         observed in. So a request written into `relay_request` is invisible to
         the cryptography domain until it is woken, and an answer published into
         `relay_reply` is invisible to the management domain until it is woken.
         Two send capabilities is what makes the handover asynchronous: each side
         writes its direction, signals, and RETURNS TO ITS EVENT LOOP.

         WHY IT IS NOT THE SIGNING DELEGATION'S SHAPE. There the asker spins for
         its answer, which works because the holder sits at a higher priority and
         has already run by the first read. That is unavailable here and would be
         wrong if it were available: a spin between two domains at priority 2
         burns the asking domain's whole slice against a domain the scheduler has
         no reason to run, on the path of every record of every handshake. Raising
         one of the two instead would put either the domain that runs adopted
         protocol code or the domain that faces the network above the dataplane's
         equal, for a session an unauthenticated peer opens. Neither is worth it,
         and neither is needed once the handover is asynchronous. THIS
         DESCRIPTION STILL HAS NO PROTECTED CALL AND GAINS NONE.

         WHAT EACH SEND CAPABILITY IS WORTH TO AN ATTACKER WHO REACHES THE OTHER
         END, which is the whole of the authority this row hands over. Reaching
         the management domain buys a wakeup on the cryptography domain at
         whatever rate they choose, plus whatever bytes they like fed to a TLS
         implementation — which is what terminating TLS for an unauthenticated
         peer IS, and it is why that code runs in a domain holding no device, no
         pipeline and no medium. Reaching the cryptography domain buys a wakeup on
         the management domain and a bounded run of bytes placed on a connection
         that domain already owns. Neither buys a key: the reply region's ABI has
         no field for one, and the private scalar is not in either of these two
         domains at all.

         BOUNDED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. One item is in flight at a time — the ABI's
         window is one and `wire::RelayRequester` refuses a second — and each side
         answers at most a constant of its own per wakeup, so a wakeup storm from
         either end costs a constant and never an unbounded loop. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="management" id="3" notify="true" />
        <end pd="crypto" id="1" notify="true" />
    </channel>

    <!-- THE CONFIGURATION DELEGATION, one-directional: the domain that terminates
         the management channel wakes the domain that decides about a document.

         WHY IT EXISTS. A configuration operation the management server pushed
         arrives in the cryptography domain, and the deciding domain blocks in the
         Microkit event loop — which is what makes it free at the highest priority
         in this system — so a request written into `chan_cfg_request` is invisible
         to it until something says so. This row is that something.

         THE REVERSE DIRECTION IS REFUSED, and `notify="false"` on the config end
         is what refuses it. It is the key holder's channel over again, with the
         roles exchanged: the asking domain reads for its answer in a BOUNDED SPIN,
         because a configuration operation happens inside a TLS session's own pass
         and has no continuation a notification could resume — so an answering
         signal would arrive after the only moment it could have been used. What a
         reverse capability WOULD be is a wakeup on the domain that holds a
         management server's session, granted to the domain that parses that
         server's documents, consumed by nothing.

         AND THAT IS WHY THE CONFIG DOMAIN SITS AT PRIORITY 3 AND THE CRYPTO DOMAIN
         AT 2. A responder BELOW its requester cannot answer a spin: the asker would
         not yield until its budget gave up, and the delegation would refuse on
         every operation, deterministically. Above it, the notification preempts
         into the decider immediately, it decides and publishes, and the asker's
         next read finds the answer — the spin terminating on its first iteration
         in practice, with its budget there for the byzantine case. Both domains
         already sat at those priorities for the management port's own reasons, so
         this row adds no priority change.

         WHAT THE SEND CAPABILITY IS WORTH TO WHOEVER TAKES THE CRYPTOGRAPHY
         DOMAIN: a wakeup on a domain that holds no device, no pool and no
         dataplane ring, plus the submission of a document that domain will judge
         against every rule it judges an administrator's by. It buys no frame, no
         medium and no key.

         BOUNDED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. One request is in flight at a time — the ABI's
         window is one — the decider answers at most one demand per wakeup, and the
         asking domain carries out AT MOST ONE configuration operation per pass,
         leaving any further frames in its decoder for the next one. So a server
         that fills a delivery with staged documents costs a constant per pass and
         never an unbounded run at a priority above the dataplane. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="crypto" id="2" notify="true" />
        <end pd="config" id="2" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- THE PERIODIC WAKEUP, one-directional, and the only channel in this file
         whose sender is not answering a peer but reporting the passage of time.

         WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. The management domain owes schedules that a silent
         link cannot drive: it re-dials its management server on a bounded
         exponential backoff, it flushes its upstream once a second while bytes
         are waiting, and it answers an acknowledgement cadence. Every one of
         those is a deadline, and until this row the only thing that entered that
         domain was a frame — so an appliance whose management server disappeared
         stopped being woken by exactly the event its backoff exists to survive.
         The domain that maps the timer block is the only one that can be told by
         hardware that an interval has elapsed, so it is the one that says so.

         WHAT THE SEND CAPABILITY IS WORTH TO WHOEVER REACHES THE CLOCK DOMAIN:
         a wakeup on the management domain at a rate they choose. That domain is
         entered by every frame its port receives already, so this buys an
         attacker who has taken the domain holding one MMIO page and two I/O
         ports a thing the network beyond the management port hands out for free
         — and it buys them nothing on the dataplane, which this row does not
         touch. What it does NOT carry is a time: the notification has no payload
         at all, and the management domain reads the calibration this domain
         published and the counter itself. An instant sent alongside would be a
         second statement of one fact, and the two would differ by however long
         the signal took.

         THE REVERSE DIRECTION IS REFUSED, and `notify="false"` on the management
         end is what refuses it. A management domain able to signal the clock
         domain would hold a wakeup capability on the domain that owns this
         node's idea of time, granted to the domain that faces the
         management-plane attacker — and it would consume nothing: the clock
         domain is woken by its own interrupt and has no work a peer could ask
         for. It is also the end that keeps `pds/nic-driver`'s claim true, the
         management domain holding send capabilities on exactly two domains and
         this being a third that is not one of them.

         BOUNDED. A notification is a flag rather than a queue, so a period that
         elapsed while the previous one was still being served is one wakeup and
         not two waiting, and the management domain's answer to a wakeup is the
         same bounded pass a frame provokes. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="clock" id="1" notify="true" />
        <end pd="management" id="4" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- THE CONSOLE DOMAIN HAS NO CHANNEL AT ALL, IN EITHER DIRECTION, AND ITS
         ABSENCE IS A DECISION.

         A DOMAIN THAT NEVER BLOCKS NEEDS NO WAKEUP. The console busy-polls the
         eight rings and never leaves `init`, for the reason set out above it, so
         it never reaches the Microkit event loop and could not observe a
         notification if one were sent. It discovers work the only way a polling
         domain can: by reading the cursor each writing domain publishes, which
         it does on every pass regardless. A send capability on this domain
         would therefore be authority granted for nothing — a widening of eight
         writers' reach with no path that consumes it — so no channel is
         declared, and the machinery that fed one is gone rather than left
         dormant.

         The direction that stays refused on its own terms: nothing here grants
         the console a send capability on any domain. A console that could
         notify a driver would hold a wakeup capability on the domain it is
         supposed to be reporting about, and a domain woken by whatever is
         printing about it is a control path dressed as a diagnostic one. That
         remains true whatever replaces the polling.

         When the console becomes interrupt-driven — the improvement recorded
         above it — the wakeup it needs is an `<irq>` from the controller, not a
         notification from a writer and not the clock domain's tick: what a
         transmitter-empty interrupt says is "the line can take more bytes",
         which is the thing the polling loop is actually waiting for. A writer's
         notification would say only "a record exists", which the cursor already
         says, and a tick would say only that time has passed, which tells this
         domain nothing about the one register it is waiting on. -->
</system>
